    "https://hr.charlotte.edu/careers/opportunities-work-unc-charlotte#process": {
      "title": "Opportunities to Work",
      "description": "Opportunities to Work at UNC Charlotte - Human Resources Skip to main content Visit the University of North Carolina at Charlotte homepage Explore Visit the University of North Carolina at Charlotte homepage Close What can we help you find? Submit search query to search.charlotte.edu Academics Admissions Campus Life Research Athletics About Apply Visit Give News & Events Directory My UNC Charlotte Quick Links Majors & Minors Graduate Programs Calendar Campus Maps Class Registration Financial Aid Housing Library Report an Incident Resources Prospective Students Alumni & Friends Faculty & Staff Current Students Parents & Family Colleges Arts + Architecture Business Computing and Informatics Education Engineering Graduate School Health and Human Services Honors College Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences Science Division of Business Affairs Human Resources Go to Search Page Search this site Search Main Menu Toggle About HR News Calendar Whom do I contact? Personnel Information Memorandums (PIMs) Careers Opportunities to Work at UNC Charlotte Dual Career Couples Employment Assistance Program For Employees Overview New Employees Student Employees Temporary Employees Equal Employment Opportunity Staff Awards and Recognition Program Employee Relations Compensation and Position Management Leaving the University? For Managers Overview Hiring Position Management Compensation Performance Management Resources for Managers Benefits Overview Benefit Plans Holidays and Leave Discounts and Perks Work Life and Wellness Learning and Organizational Development Professional Development Leadership Development Organizational Development Forms Careers Toggle Submenu Opportunities to Work at UNC Charlotte Application FAQs Dual Career Couples Employment Assistance Program Relocation Resources Opportunities to Work at UNC Charlotte Positions at UNC CHarlotte Job Board Position Types There are two categories of permanent positions at UNC Charlotte: SHRA and EHRA. Both groups have some differences in terms of policies, leave allowances and benefits. Subject to the State Human Resources Act (SHRA) SHRA employees are who are Subject to the North Carolina Human Resources Act. SHRA positions are classified into career-banded classifications based on competencies required for the position and demonstrated by the employee. Career-banded classifications and their associated salary ranges are determined by the Office of State Human Resources (OSHR). Included among these classes: accountants, administrative support, engineers, information technology, institutional services (housekeepers, grounds workers), research assistants (scientific and social), skilled trades, etc. Under the provisions of the State Human Resources Act, employees in these classes are covered by benefits (leave accrual, etc.) and job protections (progressive discipline, grievance rights, etc.) not afforded to other types of employees. Exempt from the State Human Resources Act (EHRA) EHRA employees are Exempt from the North Carolina Human Resources Act. EHRA Staff (non-faculty) positions are administrative, professional, managerial, instructional support, development, information technology, police officers and research positions. Most of these positions are “at will” employment. This means the employee serves at the will of the Chancellor and can be terminated without justification. Notice of termination is given according to the policy applicable to the particular category of EHRA Staff position. Some EHRA Staff are hired on term appointments, where a definite ending date is given, and the employee is terminated on that date without further notice. EHRA Staff positions are also normally subject to merit pay increases, as determined by legislation. Faculty The Office of Academic Budget and Personnel is responsible for the personnel process for faculty including recruitment, hiring and retention; leave and reassignment of duties requests; salary administration and special pays; and the faculty re-appointment, promotion and tenure process. More information regarding faculty policies can be found on the Academic and Budget Personnel website . Non-student Temporary Employees The need for a non-student temporary employee may be necessary when extra help is needed due to: Extended medical leaves Peak production levels Transitional period Increased workload demands Part-time non-student temporary employees are ineligible to receive benefits (leave, health coverage, State service credit, etc.). They are, however, covered by Worker’s Compensation provisions. Full-time non-student temporary employees who average 30 hours a week or more are eligible for the State Health Plan’s High Deductible Health Plan. UNC Charlotte Application Process The University offers a variety of employment opportunities for faculty, professional, staff and temporary positions (both non-student and student). The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Open Positions Each vacancy listing includes minimum eligibility requirements, essential job functions, preferred qualifications, salary range (if applicable) and closing date. Application Steps Review the following steps for SHRA, EHRA non-faculty and non-student temporary employee positions. Information on faculty positions can be found at the Academic Personnel Procedures Handbook. 1. Applications are submitted. Apply through our online system before 11:59 p.m. EST on the position’s closing date. 2. Applications are reviewed. SHRA applications are reviewed by a talent acquisition consultant in Human Resources. EHRA staff applications are reviewed by the hiring manager. 3. The interview pool is determined by the hiring manager. From this group, the interview pool is determined by an evaluation of a match to the job-related criteria, and the hiring official’s best estimation of a match to the respective responsibilities assigned to the vacant position. Please note, hiring officials are not required to interview every candidate referred to them for further consideration. Human Resources only asks that they conduct a minimum of three interviews for appropriate comparison purposes. 4. Hiring manager contacts applications for interviews. If you are selected for an interview, you will be contacted directly by the department to schedule an interview. 5. Hiring manager conducts reference checks. Once the applicant pool has been narrowed down to the final candidate, at least two reference checks must be completed. 6. Hiring proposal submitted Once the final candidate has been selected, the hiring manager completes a hiring proposal for HR to review. 7. Background check on selected candidate. To assure a safe learning and working environment for our students, faculty and staff, the Human Resources Department conducts a criminal background check of any selected candidate who: Is not a current UNC Charlotte employee, and Has been identified as the preferred candidate for a full- or part-time, permanent, time-limited or temporary position (not including student positions). Note: A conviction does not mean that you are ineligible to be hired. The nature of any offense(s)—recency, severity, and relevance—will be evaluated in relation to the job for which you are applying. All job offers are conditional upon a review of the results of the criminal background report and the authorization of an appropriate University official who has determined that no adverse action will be taken based upon the information contained in the criminal background report. Additionally, UNC Charlotte participates in the E-Verify Program as administered by the U.S. Department of Homeland Security. As a result, the University provides the Social Security Administration (SSA) and, if necessary, the Department of Homeland Security (DHS), with information from each new employee’s Form I-9 to confirm US work authorization. Note: HR does not mandate a set timetable for the completion of the interview and selection processes, but hiring decisions are typically made within four to six weeks following the posted closing date. Application Dos and Don’ts DO use our online application system: All position types require the completion of an electronic staff application or faculty/staff profile through our online application system. DO complete the application fully and ensure all relevant work experiences are included. Incomplete applications may not receive full consideration. DO only create your profile/electronic application only once. You will use your profile to apply for any and all vacancies of interest, and you can edit and modify your application as changes occur. DO submit your application on time. Applications must be submitted electronically and received no later than 11:59 p.m. (EST) on the closing date for each specific position. DO NOT type “See Resume” in the application. You may attach documents such as a cover letter, resume and references as a supplement to the required application or profile. Visit the University of North Carolina at Charlotte homepage Address 9201 University City Blvd Charlotte, NC 28223 Phone 704-687-8622 Find us on Facebook Find us on Instagram Find us on LinkedIn Find us on X Find us on YouTube Resources Alumni Association Maps & Directions Parking & Transportation My UNC Charlotte Directory Library Give Partnerships Careers Contact Us Terms of Use University Policies Health & Safety Alerts Report an Incident Emergency Response Health and Safety © 2026 UNC Charlotte. All rights reserved. HR Connect"
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/all_jobs.atom": {
      "title": "University of North Carolina",
      "description": "tag:jobs.charlotte.edu,2005:/postings/all_jobs University of North Carolina at Charlotte: All Jobs 2026-08-24T16:06:12-04:00 https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70146 2026-08-24T16:06:12-04:00 2026-08-24T16:06:12-04:00 Lab Operations Coordinator <div>This position is to manage the undergraduate biology teaching labs by ordering and preparing all materials, preparing media and maintaining cultures needed for teaching labs, managing all safety requirements, setting up laboratory experimental supplies and equipment for student use, and troubleshooting lab procedures and protocols. Must also receive and deliver daily deliveries to the mailroom/offices and to research and teaching laboratories in Klein Hall. Responsible for ensuring the proper installation, maintenance, and validation of autoclave systems, and repair of various lab equipment which may require the use of a pallet jack. This will be conducted in a safe and substantial manner, in accordance with all federal, state, and university policies. Will manage the biology prep room, which requires keeping inventory of supplies and mixing stock solutions. Must have extensive experience with standard biological equipment such as microscopes, centrifuges, autoclaves, biosafety cabinets, and water baths, etc., and knowledge of biological techniques and protocols such as microbial culture and gel electrophoresis. This person supervises student workers, assists with coordinating any teaching assistants, develops the curriculum, and communicates with the students. This person may be involved in the supervision of students in laboratory sections. The lab manager will attend TA training sessions and mandatory biohazardous training yearly.</div> Biological Sciences https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/67932 2026-05-06T15:56:05-04:00 2026-05-06T15:56:06-04:00 Adjunct Faculty/Part-Time Lecturer; Office of Student Development & Success <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Student Dev & Success https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66545 2026-02-20T08:56:14-05:00 2026-02-20T08:56:15-05:00 Part-Time Lecturer; Political Science & Public Administration <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Political Science & Public Administration https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70273 2026-08-31T16:22:17-04:00 2026-08-31T16:22:18-04:00 Assistant Director for Fraternity and Sorority Life <div>This position will oversee Well-being and Risk Management, with a large portion of this area being focused on overseeing the Standards Program, managing the collection, review, and reporting of annual assessments of chapter development, as well as working with chapters and councils to develop and maintain academic and education-focused success to increase the holistic student experience. The second area of focus will be program management with a large focus on tail-end programming for new members and graduating seniors, as well as chapter advisors. The third area of focus for this position is marketing with a design-centered approach to social media, external publication, and video production. The fourth area is student advising with oversight and guidance of two councils and the chapters that fall under those councils, ensuring success for all students and groups that fall under their purview. Council development will focus on working with student leaders to develop skills and abilities needed to lead and guide their student organization and represent the university to the fullest. The fifth portion of this role is administrative, ensuring office functions are developed and maintained with a service to the university on committee work as well as collaborations. The last area of this position is supervision, as this role will supervise both an undergraduate intern and a graduate intern.</div> Office of Fraternity&Sorority Life https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69632 2026-07-27T15:23:59-04:00 2026-07-27T15:24:00-04:00 Security Operations Specialist <div>The Security Operations Specialist is a highly technical, action-oriented role focused on defending the University’s digital infrastructure from active threats. This position will lead the initiative to integrate AI within the University's security toolset. Instead of relying on passive compliance checklists, this position leverages cutting-edge artificial intelligence, machine learning, and automated orchestration to enable proactive threat hunting, accelerate real-time threat containment, and lower alert fatigue.<br><br><strong>Security Operations Support</strong></div><ul><li>Contribute to continuous improvement of the security information and event management (SIEM) system, developing processes and procedures for monitoring, alerting, detection, and response functions for on-premise, cloud-based, and, as appropriate, third-party systems and services.</li><li>Review system logs and real-time alerts to identify trends, investigate abnormalities, and report exceptions.</li><li>Tune and create analytic detection policies for detecting and monitoring anomalous and malicious activity across the University Information Technology ecosystem.</li><li>Contribute to continuous improvement in Security Operations tasks and functions.</li><li>Support vulnerability management operations for the University and affiliates.</li><li>Create and maintain data dashboards and reports based on various systems of record to help the University IT community identify vulnerabilities, enabling them to focus their efforts to mitigate IT security risks across our environment.</li></ul><div><strong>Proactive Defense &amp; AI-Augmented SOC Support</strong></div><ul><li>Threat Hunting:<strong> </strong>Deploy machine learning models and AI-driven behavior analytics to actively hunt for hidden threat actors.</li><li>SOC Augmentation: Design and maintain AI-assisted agents to triage alerts and automate response, reducing alert fatigue and response time.</li><li>Dynamic Optimization:<strong> </strong>Continuously build, tune, and refine policies across the Security tools to accurately catch modern, fast-moving attacker techniques.</li></ul><div><strong>Engineering Offensive &amp; Defensive Tooling</strong></div><ul><li>Automated Defense: Assist Security Engineers to optimize and maintain Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR) systems.</li><li>Adversarial Simulation: Develop and automate scripts and AI attack simulations to test existing defenses.</li></ul><div><strong>Incident Response</strong></div><ul><li>Perform the role of an incident response team member in the event of a successful attack to support technical response actions, incident mitigation, and recovery activities.</li><li>Participate in investigations of security incidents and provide resolutions based on alerts and events provided by security dashboards ranging from a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system to vulnerability scans or penetration testing assessments.</li><li>Contribute to procedures for proactive and reactive Incident Response to be used University-wide.</li></ul> OneIT - Infrastructure https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70194 2026-08-26T09:18:31-04:00 2026-08-26T09:18:32-04:00 Inventory Control Specialist <div>The Inventory Control Specialist provides inventory management for all items that are procured, installed, and supported by the AV Integration and Classroom Support Teams as part of Learning Space Technology, reporting to the Digital and Professional Learning Unit. This position requires previous experience maintaining inventories and ordering and supplying necessary project items while maintaining accurate records of all procurement. The Inventory Control Specialist will monitor and track current inventory levels and equipment orders, and investigate problems with vendors to navigate supply chain demands and issues. This position will also maintain and update the database used for the physical count of inventory and the reconciliation of actual stock count to generated reports.</div><div><br></div><div>This position reports to the team that is directly responsible for managing the University’s 475+ learning spaces, common spaces, and digital signage, which requires the ordering of a minimum of $2M worth of inventory annually, tracking of each piece of equipment, and working with both Capital Projects for inventory needed for all new buildings and the Business Operations team for funding of projects. Larger capital projects for new buildings or building renovations include ordering and tracking inventory for million-dollar projects, a requirement of this position.</div> DPL-Learning Space Technology https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/68314 2026-05-29T00:01:06-04:00 2026-05-29T00:01:06-04:00 Postdoctoral Fellow, Biological Sciences_Reitzel lab <div>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs' aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees.<br><br>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions:<br><br>* the appointee was recently (within the last eight years) awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.);<br>* the appointment is temporary;<br>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;<br>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;<br>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and<br>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.<br><br></div> Biological Sciences https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66566 2026-02-23T09:56:58-05:00 2026-02-23T09:56:59-05:00 Adjunct / Part-Time Faculty; School of Nursing <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, supervision of students in clinical settings, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> School of Nursing https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70287 2026-09-01T07:55:08-04:00 2026-09-01T07:55:08-04:00 Assistant Director of Business Services for HRL <div>The Assistant Director of Business Services supports the Director of Business Services in administering the financial, personnel, and operational functions of Housing and Residence Life within a complex auxiliary environment. This position assists with managing a unified departmental budget exceeding $57 million and supports financial planning efforts associated with ongoing capital renovation and refurbishment projects totaling more than $70 million. The role supports budget development, financial forecasting, expenditure tracking, auditing, financial reporting, and transaction processing while utilizing enterprise systems such as Banner, Report Central, Tableau, Concur, NinerTalent, and NinerWorks. The position also analyzes financial and operational data to identify trends, discrepancies, and opportunities for process improvement.</div><div><br>Additionally, this position serves as a liaison to Human Resources and Employee Relations by providing guidance to supervisors and hiring managers on recruitment, onboarding, and offboarding, position management, performance management, and employee conduct matters in alignment with university and state policies. The Assistant Director works collaboratively with the Director of Business Services to support the supervision and operational oversight of the department’s Security Guard Supervisors and assists in ensuring operational continuity, compliance, and effective personnel management for the overnight security operation. The role also assists with vendor relations, contract administration, operational communications, and collaboration with campus partners to support divisional goals and business operations.</div> Housing and Residence Life https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69679 2026-07-29T14:44:09-04:00 2026-07-29T14:44:10-04:00 Associate Director of Affinity Engagement <div><strong>Program Management</strong></div><ul><li>The Associate Director of Alumni Engagement is responsible for managing affinity-based alumni events and programs. Affinity-based events and programs are defined as events or programs organized by and for groups of alumni with a shared interest or experience. Events may be in person, on or off campus, as well as virtual. Affinity examples include, but are not limited to, geographic location, corporate affiliation, student organization affiliation, and professional industry. This position will strategically develop programs, activities and communications to connect, serve and engage alumni within various affinities.</li><li>As part of a university-wide corporate engagement strategy, this position will support the Senior Associate Director of Affinity Networks to create and execute engagement opportunities for alumni working within key Charlotte area corporations.</li><li>This position will be responsible for building engagement strategies with Greek alumni chapters and Greek-affiliated alumni to build relationships and channels for exchanging data and creating opportunities for engagement. This position will also work to identify and provide campus resources and guidance for their annual events.</li><li>This position is also responsible for serving as the Alumni Engagement liaison with the Athletics department and will manage athletics-related events such as watch parties, away game tailgates, and conference tournament events.</li><li>The Associate Director will collaborate with the Alumni Communications Specialist to ensure that the communications and marketing initiatives are aligned and successfully coordinated with their programming area. The position will work with the specialist to develop a yearly communications plan.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Volunteer Management</strong></div><ul><li>The position is responsible for managing volunteers and committees working on specific programs or events, such as corporate engagement activities, Greek organization alumni gatherings, regional gatherings, athletic watch parties, away game tailgates, and other affinity-based events. This position will respond to alumni inquiries and proactively identify alumni groups to engage.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Fundraising</strong></div><ul><li>Work with volunteer boards to reach 100% giving by members during each fiscal year. This may require creating and sending letters and/or email solicitations and direct verbal asks.<br><br></li></ul><div><strong>Other Work Responsibilities</strong></div><ul><li>This position will conduct monthly one-on-one meetings with alumni to deepen awareness about involvement opportunities and strengthen their connection with the University. Associate Director will be responsible for developing and managing a budget for their area of oversight.</li><li>Associate Director will exercise sound judgment in selecting event venues and vendors. The position will assess vendors and venues from a risk management perspective in order to ensure that the university’s safety standards are met.</li><li>Other duties as assigned.</li></ul> Alumni Engagement https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/64355 2025-09-24T00:00:58-04:00 2025-10-16T12:07:23-04:00 Post-Doctoral Fellow <div><br></div><ol><li>Perform fundamental theoretical and experimental research on LPBF and binder jetting of metallic/ceramic materials</li><li>Plan and execute intricate experiments, and systematically gather as well as analyze the resulting multimodal datasets</li><li>Formulate and integrate constitutive models for LPBF and binder jetting AM into FE codes for application in process optimization studies</li><li>Utilize ML algorithms and optimization frameworks in conjunction with FE simulations to develop process optimization framework for metal/ceramic AM</li><li>Collaborate with fellow researchers and technical staff to discuss simulation, production, and material characterization</li><li>Author and publish original research articles in peer-reviewed journals</li></ol><div><br></div> Mechanical Engineering and Engineering Science https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70200 2026-08-26T10:44:04-04:00 2026-08-26T10:44:05-04:00 Temporary Research Support Pool-Hourly <div>* Responsibilities may include: conducting experiments, collecting and summarizing data, and providing proper laboratory/study subject care.<br><br>* Duties will vary by departmental needs.</div> All departments https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/68644 2026-06-12T10:32:04-04:00 2026-06-12T10:32:04-04:00 Post Doctoral Fellow; EPIC <div>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs' aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees.<br><br>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions:<br><br>* the appointee was recently (within the last eight years) awarded a Ph.D. Electrical Engineering or a closely related field, a strong background in power electronics, and experience with experimental research.<br>* the appointment is temporary;<br>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;<br>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;<br>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and<br>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.</div> College of Engineering https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66909 2026-03-10T15:38:16-04:00 2026-03-10T15:38:17-04:00 Adjunct/Part Time Faculty; Educational Leadership <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Educational Leadership https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70300 2026-09-01T14:18:11-04:00 2026-09-01T14:18:12-04:00 Administrative Support Specialist <ul><li>This position audits/processes purchase order invoices from all University funding sources (State Appropriation, Federal Grants, Special Instructional and Auxiliary) using Banner, 49er Mart, and an imaging system.</li><li>Issues payments to vendors and reimbursements for expenses incurred by employees, non-employees, and students in compliance with Federal, State, and University policies, guidelines, and acceptable risks.</li></ul> Controller https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69741 2026-08-04T18:08:25-04:00 2026-08-04T18:08:26-04:00 Cybersecurity, Digital Engineering & AI Innovation Lab & Facility Manager <div>The Cybersecurity, Digital Engineering &amp; AI Innovation Lab &amp; Facility Manager provides operational leadership for the lab and facility. This role is the primary technical and administrative lead for the facility and serves as the essential link between the University, Division of Research, and the North Tryon Tech Hub. The position focuses on managing sophisticated research environments, overseeing building operations, and facilitating technical engagement with industry partners and regional initiatives. The manager has direct responsibility for the facility, including the digital infrastructure required for modern engineering workflows, and holds master key access to secure areas.<br><br></div><div>● <strong>Research Lab Facility &amp; Building Operations - 50%</strong></div><div>● <strong>Technical Innovation &amp; Support - 20%</strong></div><div>● <strong>Planning and Organizing Work - 10%</strong></div><div>● <strong>Administrative &amp; Fiscal Management - 20%<br><br>Research Lab Facility &amp; Building Operations<br></strong>● <strong>Operational Oversight:</strong> Manage daily activities of all classrooms, labs, and specialized structures, ensuring 24/7 operational readiness for sensitive data, AI workloads, and Digital Engineering simulations.</div><div>● <strong>Building Liaison:</strong> Act as the lead coordinator and liaison to University Facilities Management for all building/lab infrastructure needs, including high-capacity power, specialized cooling for AI clusters, and high-bandwidth data networking.</div><div>● <strong>Space &amp; Access Management:</strong> Oversee all lab and office assignments; manage advanced security protocols and building access for faculty and industry collaborators using systems like Archibus.</div><div>● <strong>Inventory &amp; Security: </strong>Serve as the primary inventory manager for all equipment; maintain records for both physical devices and virtualized assets/software licenses required for Digital Engineering.</div><div>● <strong>Outreach:</strong> Organize and direct outreach activities, including tours and demonstrations of Digital Twin models and AI-driven research for industry partners and research sponsors.<br><br></div><div><strong>Technical Innovation &amp; Support<br></strong>● <strong>Digital Engineering Integration: </strong>Oversee the deployment and maintenance of Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) tools and collaborative digital environments that allow for the virtual design, testing, and validation of complex systems.</div><div>● <strong>Expert Troubleshooting:</strong> Oversee the maintenance and repair of complex electronic, mechanical, and data systems; provide tier-one support for Digital Engineering software suites and high-performance computing (HPC) interfaces.</div><div>● <strong>Safety &amp; Compliance:</strong> Establish and enforce rigorous safety standards tailored to the unique risks of the lab (e.g., technical systems, high-voltage data centers, and cyber-physical security).<br><br></div><div><strong>Planning and Organizing Work<br></strong>● <strong>Strategic Planning: </strong>Assist leadership in determining long-range plans for core facilities based on strategic assessments of growth opportunities in digital transformation and AI, in alignment with the Division of Research strategic plan.</div><div>● <strong>Modernization Planning:</strong> Develop 3-year rolling plans for equipment staging, facility retrofitting, and digital toolchain upgrades to keep the Innovation Lab at the forefront of the Tech Hub's requirements. Evaluate performance and cost options for system upgrades to maintain a “State-of-the-Art” status in virtual engineering environments.</div><div>● <strong>Safety &amp; Security:</strong> Serve as the building liaison and safety officer, coordinating security protocols for both physical assets and intellectual property stored within the digital lab environment.<br><br></div><div><strong>Administrative &amp; Fiscal Management<br></strong>● <strong>Budgetary Authority:</strong> Manage the overall lab operations budget and develop justifications for major capital equipment purchases and enterprise-level software subscriptions.</div><div>● <strong>Procurement &amp; Vendor Relations:</strong> Serve as the primary contact for vendors; evaluate quotes for new equipment, cloud service providers, service contracts, delivery schedules, and specialized installations.</div> Office of Research Services https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69007 2026-06-29T09:37:14-04:00 2026-06-29T09:37:15-04:00 Translation Program Director <ul><li><strong>Conducts</strong> proactive, campus-wide engagement with UNC Charlotte researchers — in research centers, institutes, and directly with individual researchers not affiliated with any center — to identify research with commercial translation potential at TRL 2–3, before it self-selects through formal disclosure or exits through publication unrealized.</li><li><strong>Facilitates</strong> structured problem-framing sessions between researchers and Charlotte industry counterparts, translating scientific findings into operational problem statements that corporate partners in energy, advanced manufacturing, autonomous vehicles, aerospace, defense and intelligence, and optics recognize as real and worth solving.</li><li><strong>Evaluates</strong> the translation readiness of technologies in the assigned portfolio by conducting Technical Readiness Level (TRL), Adoption Readiness Level (ARL), and Manufacturing Readiness Level (MRL) assessments, and prepares the Translation Validation Clearance documentation required for handoff to New Ventures &amp; Industry Alliances.</li><li><strong>Cultivates</strong> and sustains direct, working-level relationships with VP and Director-level counterparts in corporate R&amp;D and engineering at Charlotte's corporate partners and with C-level leadership at SMBs in assigned domains, drawing on personal industry expertise and networks to make peer-level introductions between faculty inventors and potential corporate partners and investors.</li><li><strong>Coordinates</strong> across all three ORTIA units — Translation Intelligence, New Ventures &amp; Industry Alliances, and IP &amp; Technology Transfer — exercising cross-unit convening authority as designated by the AVC to move technologies through the translation pipeline from initial identification through Translation Validation Clearance and handoff.</li><li><strong>Proposes</strong> Mentor-in-Residence matches for technologies at TRL 3–5 in the assigned portfolio, identifying experienced entrepreneurs and former corporate executives from Charlotte's business community whose domain background and operational experience align with the technology and the faculty inventor.</li><li><strong>Supports</strong> federal and state commercialization programs — including NSF I-Corps, NSF Tech Scout, and internal grant programs, preparing them for application, and ensuring program outputs are integrated into ORTIA's translation pipeline.</li><li><strong>Maintains</strong> accurate and current documentation of all researchers and technology engagements in ORTIA's pipeline tracker, and participates in weekly pipeline coordination meetings to report portfolio status, identify technologies at handoff points, and surface resource needs across units for resolution by the AVC.</li></ul> Res Trans & Indust Alliance (ORTIA) https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/64918 2025-11-01T00:01:46-04:00 2025-11-01T00:01:46-04:00 Postdoctoral Fellow in Suicide Prevention Training <div>This position is not remote-eligible. The holder of the postdoctoral fellowship will:</div><ol><li>Coordinate multi-site team meetings and communication.</li><li>Oversee feasibility/acceptability data collection efforts in coordination with Ohio State University partners.</li><li>Prepare datasets for and support the execution of data analysis.</li><li>Co-author project-related institutional review board (IRB) and other regulatory documents.</li><li>Co-author project-related scholarly and other outputs.</li><li>Co-develop training materials and recordings the suicide prevention training program.</li><li>Participate in weekly supervision and mentoring meetings with the project PI Dr. Cramer.</li><li>Participate in periodic co-supervision meetings with project Co-Is (Dr. Sam Cacace, Dr. Jessamyn Moxie, Dr. Annelise Mennicke).</li><li>Conduct an individualized mentoring plan (IMP) to establish their own research agenda within the Violence Prevention Center.</li><li>Support Violence Prevention Center research and operations.</li><li>Participate in other interprofessional team science research in areas such as suicide prevention, military health, sexual and gender minority (SGM) health, violence prevention, and/or other areas.</li><li>Participate in the UNC Charlotte Department of Public Health and Health Administration meetings and professional development opportunities.</li></ol><div>As an EOE/AA employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained, the University of North Carolina at Charlotte encourages applications from all underrepresented groups. Applicants subject to criminal background check.<br><br>The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree.<br><br></div> Public Hlth & Hlth Admin https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70221 2026-08-28T07:51:54-04:00 2026-08-28T07:51:54-04:00 Temporary Financial Aid Assistant <ul><li>Manage daily, weekly, and monthly reports associated with the Office of Financial Aid involving but not limited to awarding, residency, unusual enrollment, student semester enrollment, Federal Pell Grants, Federal TEACH Grant, and Transient Study processing.</li><li>Performs specialized duties to support Pell Grant and TEACH Grant programs.</li><li>Provides accurate and timely information about financial aid policies and procedures to current and prospective students, their families, and other campus visitors through individual meetings and outreach.</li><li>Analyze and review output reports for awarding and disbursement accuracy.</li><li>Resolve discrepancies.</li><li>Coordinate with other institutions to resolve Pell MRR’s.</li><li>Process and monitor the Transient Study Program.</li><li>Other duties within the scope of the role may be assigned as operational needs evolve.</li></ul> Financial Aid Operations https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66914 2026-03-10T15:44:46-04:00 2026-03-10T15:44:46-04:00 Adjunct/Part Time Faculty; Middle, Secondary, K-12 Education <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Middle Grades, Secondary & K-12 https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70321 2026-09-02T12:21:08-04:00 2026-09-02T12:21:09-04:00 Alumni Communications Specialist <ul><li>Collaborate with Program Managers to develop tailored email marketing strategies for alumni events, defining audience targets, messaging angles, and send schedules.</li><li>Write, design, and proofread engaging, brand-aligned email communications to promote alumni events, programs, and department initiatives. Design custom graphics as needed and select high-quality visual assets to elevate email aesthetics and drive engagement.</li><li>Coordinate with Program Managers and the Senior Director of Alumni Engagement to ensure all messaging and recipient lists are thoroughly proofread, approved, and deployed error-free on schedule.</li><li>Define and recommend targeted alumni audience segments in collaboration with Program Managers to maximize engagement and attendance.</li><li>Collaborate closely with University Communications to align alumni messaging with overall institutional brand standards and strategic goals.</li><li>Serve as the team lead on email marketing standards, continually recommending improvements for design, layout, tone, call-to-action placement, and subject lines.</li><li>Serve as the resident subject matter expert for the Cvent event management platform and master email marketing workflows within Cvent.</li><li>Build, verify, and pull targeted invitation lists and detailed reports directly from Salesforce while maintaining clean list practices and data integrity.</li><li>Track, analyze, and report on key email performance metrics (open rates, CTRs, conversion rates, unsubscribes) to refine future strategies.</li><li>Conduct background research on annual Alumni Award recipients to highlight their achievements and impact.</li><li>Lead the interview process for Alumni Award recipients, developing tailored, insightful video script questions. Collaborate with the Director of Alumni Operations and Special Projects to guide recipients through video production interviews.</li><li>As needed, draft remarks for Department leadership for high-profile events including, but not limited to, Alumni Awards, 10 Under Ten Awards, ACE Awards &amp; Appreciation Luncheon and Legacy Lunch.</li><li>Provide key email communication support for university-wide engagement and fundraising campaigns, such as Niner Nation Gives and Niner Nation Week.</li><li>Independently manage communication calendars, prioritizing multiple concurrent projects to ensure all emails are drafted, approved, and deployed on schedule.</li><li>Ability to work some weekends and evenings; occasional travel may be required.</li><li>Other duties as assigned</li></ul> University Advancement VC https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69865 2026-08-12T10:55:47-04:00 2026-08-12T10:55:47-04:00 Adjunct/Part Time Faculty; School of Data Science <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> School of Data Science (SDS) https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69078 2026-07-01T00:01:21-04:00 2026-07-22T08:25:46-04:00 Temporary Clerical Pool-Salaried <div>* Responsibilities may include: general office reception, mail distribution, records processing, data entry, filing, correspondence, appointment scheduling, general accounting, etc.<br><br>* Duties will vary by departmental needs.</div> All departments https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/64995 2025-11-07T13:24:28-05:00 2025-11-07T13:24:29-05:00 Adjunct/Part Time Lecturer; Computer Science <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Computer Science https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70231 2026-08-28T12:06:58-04:00 2026-08-28T12:06:58-04:00 Building Operations Assistant <div>Assist with the library daily operations. Duties include, recycling, painting, changing lights, replacing ceiling tiles, mail room coverage, moving furniture, book donations pickups and deliveries on and off campus, cleaning furniture, repairing furniture, responding to emergency leaks, carpet spot cleaning and replacement, window cleaning, elevator track cleaning, office cleaning, special event setup, computer and printer setup, maintain safe and well organized storage areas, relocate personnel within the building and assist with fixed assets. Clean and maintains all equipment used to upkeep the Library. Other duties as assigned.</div> Library https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66916 2026-03-10T15:48:03-04:00 2026-03-10T15:48:04-04:00 Adjunct/Part Time Faculty; Special Education & Child Development <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Special Ed & Child Dev https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70328 2026-09-02T15:45:08-04:00 2026-09-02T15:45:08-04:00 Chief Executive Officer - Grid Modernization Engine in the Carolinas <div><strong>Strategic Leadership &amp; Organizational Growth</strong></div><ul><li>Drive the overall vision, strategy, and execution to build and scale a high-impact grid modernization and energy transition initiative.</li><li>Establish management operating structures and internal processes to support rapid organizational scaling and long-term sustainability.</li><li>Recruit, retain, and mentor a high-performing leadership team within a technically driven, innovation-focused environment.</li><li>Direct federal compliance and financial oversight and reporting to satisfy complex federal funding and regulatory requirements.</li></ul><div><strong>Partnership Building &amp; Stakeholder Management</strong></div><ul><li>Cultivate and manage multi-sector coalitions across academia, electric utilities, government agencies, private industry, and non-profit organizations around a shared regional vision.</li><li>Serve as the primary liaison to the Governance Board, advisory councils, and committees, aligning diverse stakeholder interests with organizational priorities.</li><li>Build and maintain trusting relationships with regional funders, elected officials, and community leaders to strengthen institutional standing.</li></ul><div><strong>Technology Commercialization &amp; Innovation Deployment</strong></div><ul><li>Oversee the transition of early-stage grid technology research into scalable, market-ready products and commercial solutions.</li><li>Align academic R&amp;D initiatives with utility needs and market demands to accelerate technology adoption across the energy sector.</li><li>Establish metrics and reporting structures to measure the impact of commercialization efforts on regional economic development, supply chain growth, and workforce readiness.</li></ul><div><strong>Resource Development &amp; External Representation</strong></div><ul><li>Act as the chief external spokesperson at regional and national forums, industry conferences, and high-level stakeholder convenings.</li><li>Identify and pursue outside investment to continually expand the initiative's programs and regional footprint.</li></ul> Div of Research VC Office https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70046 2026-08-19T08:59:24-04:00 2026-08-19T08:59:25-04:00 Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (open rank) <div><br>The Director is responsible for all Center programs and, in collaboration with university faculty and personnel in regional schools and state and national organizations, plays a leadership role in securing external funding to support STEM education research, projects, and programs. The Director also provides direct supervision and leadership for Center personnel, including the Associate Director, Pre-College Program Director, Assistant Pre-College Program Coordinator, Office Manager, and administrative support associates (Assistant Coordinator), ensuring effective staffing, professional development, performance management, and alignment of staff responsibilities with Center goals. Working with university faculty; PK–12 teachers; staff in the North Carolina Department of Public Instruction; members of state, national, and international professional societies; staff in local, state, and national agencies; and other stakeholders, the Director’s responsibilities include the following:</div><ol><li>Teacher Professional Development: Develop and implement professional development programs in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics for teachers in grades PK–12.</li><li>Student Academic Enrichment: Provide STEM academic enrichment opportunities for PK–20 students in North Carolina that improve preparation and achievement in STEM disciplines, particularly among students not yet prepared to pursue those fields, while also encouraging academically talented students.</li><li>Active Research Agenda: As the Center is integral to the University’s designation as a Research 1 institution, the Director is expected to develop and implement a research agenda aligned with CSTEM priorities.</li><li>External Funding: Develop funding proposals in collaboration with campus faculty; PK–12 educators; faculty across the UNC system and other state, national, and international institutions; researchers and staff in partner organizations; and community-based and informal education groups to secure external funding for STEM teacher professional development, curriculum and materials development, and student academic enrichment programs and competitions.</li><li>Visibility that Links with Related Organizations: Develop and implement ongoing strategies to promote the mission, goals, and resources of CSTEM.</li></ol> Ctr For STEM Education https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69089 2026-07-01T15:01:13-04:00 2026-07-01T15:01:13-04:00 Postdoctoral Research Associate (Hokmabadi) <div>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs' aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees.<br><br>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions:<br><br>* the appointee was recently (within the last eight years) awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.);<br>* the appointment is temporary, for up to one year at a time, with renewal possible;<br>* the total appointment is limited to five years, including time spent in previous postdoctoral appointments at other institutions;<br>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;<br>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;<br>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and<br>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.<br><br><br><br></div> Electrical & Computer Engineering https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66339 2026-02-06T11:46:38-05:00 2026-02-06T11:46:39-05:00 Adjunct / Part-Time Faculty; Appl Phys Hlth & Clin Sci <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Appl Phys Hlth & Clin Sci https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70249 2026-08-31T10:36:11-04:00 2026-08-31T10:36:11-04:00 Clinical Professor & NC Economic Impact Center Director <div>The Department of Economics in the Belk College of Business at UNC Charlotte is seeking to fill a Clinical Professor position. The position involves both teaching and administrative responsibilities as Director of the newly established North Carolina Economic Impact Center. The candidate will be on a reduced teaching load of four sections during the academic year due to the Director duties. Specific courses taught will be determined in consultation with the Department Chair.<br><br></div><div>The Director of the North Carolina Economic Impact Center will be responsible for administrative oversight of the Center. The primary purpose of the Center is to engage with industry and community leaders in the greater Charlotte region and conduct economic analyses by securing external funding.<br><br></div><div>Responsibilities for this position include sufficient academic and/or other professional activities to meet the Belk College Faculty Qualification Standards. Will also engage in appropriate service activities in the Department, College, University and Charlotte community.</div> Economics https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70060 2026-08-19T14:56:24-04:00 2026-08-19T14:56:25-04:00 Senior HPC Systems Specialist <div>This Senior HPC Systems Specialist serves as a key team member reporting to the Director of University Research Computing. This position performs critical systems management and other operational duties, with responsibilities and objectives delegated by the Director. This position contributes to shaping the strategic direction of University Research Computing. At times, this position may provide leadership or supervision of the technical work of other staff members.<br>This position is responsible for supporting the ongoing operation of the Research Computing facilities, including systems management and maintenance, developing and deploying upgraded capability, and interacting with and supporting users. The position is also responsible for supporting technical design and strategy development. The position also includes responsibility for directing the technical work of other members of staff.</div> OneIT - Research Computing https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/67191 2026-03-25T08:23:44-04:00 2026-03-25T08:23:44-04:00 Adjunct/Part-Time Faculty; Department of Writing, Rhetoric & Digital Studies <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> DPT Writing, Rhet, Digital Studies https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70338 2026-09-03T13:17:35-04:00 2026-09-03T13:17:36-04:00 Academic Advisor I <div>The Academic Advisor in the College of Computing and Informatics (CCI) will assume advising responsibilities for any University students declaring or expressing an interest in College of Computing and Informatics majors. The advisor must be able to work autonomously in a complex, team-oriented academic office with a significant advising caseload. The advisor must be committed to student success and able to listen to and analyze academic needs. The advisor must also relate well to faculty and staff and provide support to them as requested.<br><br>While not required to teach, Academic Advisors in CCI may be requested to coordinate and/or participate in seminars offered each year to students. In addition, the advisor fulfills other responsibilities as needed and assigned by the Director of CCI Advising.<br>Specific functional areas include:<br>• Academic advising<br>• Orientation and outreach<br>• Targeted advising<br>• Instruction<br>• Other duties as assigned</div> College of Computing & Informatics https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69243 2026-07-07T13:52:08-04:00 2026-07-07T13:52:09-04:00 Senior Classification/Compensation Consultant <div>UNC Charlotte is seeking a highly skilled, independent, and strategic Senior Classification and Compensation Consultant to drive excellence across the university’s workforce infrastructure. In this pivotal role, the Senior Classification and Compensation Consultant balances deep regulatory expertise with proactive campus partnership, focusing heavily on sophisticated compensation and job analysis. This involves independently evaluating complex position classifications, determining FLSA and SHRA/EHRA exemption statuses, performing market and internal equity reviews, and providing vital organizational design recommendations. Additionally, the Senior Classification and Compensation Consultant serves as a trusted advisor to administrators, faculty, and HR partners on effective job design, resolving sensitive or unprecedented compensation challenges through targeted research, and offering guidance and mentorship to fellow team members.<br><br></div><div>Beyond daily analysis and consultation, the Senior Classification and Compensation Consultant ensures strict compliance with federal, state, and local compensation laws. In this capacity, the Consultant conducts thorough audits, prepares data-driven metrics for leadership, and supports critical cyclical initiatives such as the annual raise process, salary surveys, and UNC System Office reporting. Finally, the Senior Classification and Compensation Consultant leverages communication expertise to mitigate institutional risk and empower campus partners through the development and facilitation of compelling, ongoing training programs and educational workshops. Experienced compensation professionals looking to make a visible impact on talent retention and operational effectiveness within a dynamic higher education environment are invited to apply.<br><br></div><ul><li>Performs market and equity analysis of positions to assess salaries and proactively addresses compensation issues.</li><li>Analyzes market data to assess range updates and compensation administration to ensure competitive compensation practices.</li><li>Performance job analysis, determines classifications and salary ranges, provides organizational analysis and design recommendations, creates new positions, updates information on positions changes, and determines exemption status (FLSA and SHRA/EHRA).</li><li>Expected to act independently and be knowledgeable of the state classification system, its processes and procedures.</li><li>Advises campus and HR partners on compensation matters.</li><li>Offers guidance and advice on the design of positions and their placement within the organization. Provides guidance to other team members.</li><li>Interprets policies, procedures, and best practices and explains how they are applied within the context of classification and compensation analysis.</li><li>Collaborates with customers and internal team to resolve unique and/or sensitive issues which requires research and developing options for successfully addressing campus partner and organizational needs.</li><li>Obtains position comparisons internally and externally.</li><li>Ensure compliance with federal, state, and local compensation laws and regulations. Prepare and present reports on compensation trends and metrics to Director of Position Management.</li></ul> Compensation and Position Management https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66425 2026-02-12T11:14:12-05:00 2026-02-12T11:14:13-05:00 Adjunct/Part-time Faculty; Management <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Management https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70260 2026-08-31T14:41:50-04:00 2026-08-31T14:41:51-04:00 Scholarship Administration Coordinator <ul><li>Serve as the primary contact for scholarship administrators and other departmental representatives seeking training, technical assistance, and resources that support scholarship administration.</li><li>Understand and have the ability to clearly explain how scholarship donor agreements and funds are established, how to calculate scholarship fund balances, and what the functions are within the scholarship application, review, and award cycles.</li><li>Demonstrate advanced knowledge, experience, and the ability to quickly learn content management systems designed for scholarship and fund management.</li><li>Develop and maintain positive working relationships with scholarship administrators and other departmental personnel responsible for managing scholarship funds, reviewing applications, and awarding scholarships.</li><li>Provide initial and ongoing training and support to university personnel responsible for scholarship fund administration and use of the scholarship management system.</li><li>Develop training content and resource materials for users, and lead implementation of the annual Scholarship Administration Training Session.</li><li>Present to large and small audiences that attend general information and training sessions, or other public events related to scholarship administration.</li><li>Develop and maintain scholarship management user guides that provide policy and system guidance for administrators, reviewers, and students.</li><li>Respond to requests for information or support from college or departmental personnel in a timely manner. Prepare reports and other resources to satisfy the inquiry as needed.</li><li>Track scholarship fund balances, offers, and award payments for departments outside the USO to ensure all scholarship funding available is being awarded in compliance with the terms of donor agreements.</li><li>Raise awareness of the mission and resources provided by the University Scholarship Office, explain university processes and policies related to effective scholarship administration, troubleshoot scholarship system, program, and fund issues.</li></ul> University Scholarship Office https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70126 2026-08-21T15:54:24-04:00 2026-08-21T15:54:25-04:00 Academic Advisor I <div>The Academic Advisor will assume advising responsibilities for any assigned student within the Klein College of Science. This individual will also be responsible for supporting students declaring or expressing interest in majors and minors offered by the Klein College of Science. The advisor must have the ability to work autonomously in a complex, team-oriented academic office while also building strong alliances with faculty and other campus partners that support the holistic development of students. The advisor must have a commitment to student success and the ability to listen and analyze academic needs.<br><br>The Academic Advisor will also be engaged in other activities that support the orientation, onboarding, and consistent communication to UNC Charlotte students. These activities might include teaching and seminar facilitation, event participation, coordinated messaging, and other duties as assigned.</div> Col of Science https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/67704 2026-04-22T09:20:17-04:00 2026-04-22T09:20:18-04:00 Building Envelope Architect/Engineer <div><strong>Envelope Authority:</strong><br>Apply advanced building science expertise to govern University envelope standards; establish and enforce Design and Construction Manual (DCM) standards for both the exterior skin and high-durability interior finishes.<br><br></div><div><strong>Technical Review &amp; Forensics:<br></strong>Direct the Building Enclosure Commissioning (BECx) process for capital projects; authorize performance testing (infrared thermography, air barrier testing) and conduct forensic analysis on existing facility failures (e.g., water intrusion, chronic elevator outages).<br><br></div><div><strong>Asset &amp; Maintenance Liaison:</strong><br>Formulate the Asset Management Plan for the building shell, interiors, and vertical transportation; translate complex engineering requirements into practical workflows for Maintenance Operations shops.<br><br></div><div><strong>Consultation &amp; Strategic Liaison:<br></strong>Serve as the primary technical resource for maintaining fire ratings and thermal boundaries during renovations; negotiate with external architects to ensure aesthetic choices do not override maintenance durability.<br><br></div><div><strong>Strategic Leadership:</strong><br>Champion the \"Total Building Performance\" strategy to identify and correct disconnects between initial building design and long-term maintenance costs.</div> Engineering Services https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70352 2026-09-04T15:02:31-04:00 2026-09-04T15:02:32-04:00 Library Communications Specialist <div>The Library Communications Specialist will be responsible for a variety of tasks that contribute to engagement, effective communications, marketing, and outreach activities for the library. The position will work with the director to create, maintain, and analyze content for the library’s digital media, website, and printed materials. The position will support and, in some cases, lead the execution of library events. The incumbent also supports programs, special projects, and the logistics of marketing efforts. The Library Communications Specialist:</div><ul><li>Serves on the Student Engagement Committee</li><li>Works closely with Administration on logistics of events and purchasing.</li><li>Manages the promotion of the library’s student outreach efforts, including De-Stress for Success, NSO, and Gold Rush.</li><li>Assists the Director of Library Communications with the creation, proofreading, and editing of clear marketing messages that are appropriate for the intended audiences.</li><li>Assists with website content production and management.</li><li>Responsible for digital signage content management.</li><li>Under the supervision of the director, creates and maintains social media platforms and efforts; composes, develops, and schedules posts for the Library’s social media channels; monitors outreach and activity generated by social media posts.</li><li>Works with vendors to order printing and promotional items. Maintains collateral materials, promotional items and supplies, and fulfills requests.</li><li>Assists with interdepartmental projects.</li><li>Takes photos at events and in the building as needed.</li><li>Works closely with the graphic designer to produce a variety of promotional materials.</li></ul> Library https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69357 2026-07-10T14:59:15-04:00 2026-07-10T14:59:15-04:00 Adjunct/Part-Time Faculty; Women's and Gender Studies <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Interdisciplinary Studies https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66428 2026-02-12T11:26:15-05:00 2026-02-12T11:26:15-05:00 Adjunct/Part-Time Faculty; BISOM <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Business Info Systems/Operations https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70267 2026-08-31T15:21:01-04:00 2026-08-31T15:21:02-04:00 Transfer Advisor <ul><li>Provide academic advising to prospective and enrolled transfer and adult students through in-person and virtual appointments and other advising modalities.</li><li>Maintain an assigned caseload of 49erNext students enrolled at North Carolina community colleges and provide proactive pre-transfer advising and follow-up.</li><li>Assist students in understanding how prior credit may apply to degree requirements, including general education, major prerequisites, and course sequencing.</li><li>Guide students in exploring academic options and selecting coursework prior to enrollment that supports preparation for their intended major and timely progression toward graduation.</li><li>Support students in navigating academic policies and processes, including major admission and declaration requirements, change of major processes, and institutional expectations after transfer.</li><li>Provide supplemental advising support to enrolled students with transfer credit, including guidance on the academic petition process for credit reevaluation.</li><li>Support newly enrolled students in navigating their transition and connecting with appropriate campus resources and services.</li><li>Maintain accurate and timely documentation of advising interactions in accordance with university policies and best practices.</li><li>Develop and facilitate group advising sessions and other scalable opportunities that expand access to pre-transfer guidance for all prospective UNC Charlotte students, including students participating in 49erNext.</li><li>Participate in outreach efforts, including partner visits, campus events, and virtual engagement opportunities.</li><li>Assist with engagement and transition programming for transfer initiatives, including 49erNext Scholars, to help incoming students connect with campus resources, understand academic expectations, and build community.</li><li>Use student questions, advising trends, participation data, and feedback to identify opportunities for new resources and improvements to advising and programming.</li><li>Monitor and respond to inquiries received through Center email accounts and other communication channels.</li><li>Provide timely, accurate, and student-centered information to prospective and enrolled students.</li><li>Assist with the development and maintenance of advising resources, program communications, and website content.</li><li>Support data collection and reporting related to advising, resource use, program participation, and student engagement.</li><li>Participate in departmental and university initiatives related to transfer, adult learner success, student transition, and pathway development.</li><li>Collaborate with campus and external partners to support transfer pathways, improve the student experience, and reduce credit loss.</li><li>Serve on committees or working groups as appropriate.</li><li>Engage in ongoing professional development to stay current with best practices in advising, transfer, and adult student support.</li></ul> Univ Transfer/Adult Stu Ctr https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70145 2026-08-24T16:04:24-04:00 2026-08-24T16:04:25-04:00 Audio Visual Coordinator <ul><li>Actively participates and assists the Assistant Director, A/V &amp; Tech Services in the management of the audio-visual services program for the Student Centers.</li><li>Serve as an audio-visual staff member on site for major and/or special events as determined by the supervisor, with a focus on evening and weekend activities (e.g., Chancellor-related events, Homecoming, etc.).</li><li>Under the direction of the Assistant Director, A/V &amp; Tech Services, hire, train, schedule, and manage the Audio Visual Technician student staff.</li></ul> Student Union https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/67883 2026-05-05T07:55:02-04:00 2026-05-05T07:55:02-04:00 Adjunct/Part Time Faculty; Public Health and Health Administration <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Public Hlth & Hlth Admin https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70270 2026-08-31T15:39:51-04:00 2026-08-31T15:39:52-04:00 Assistant Director for Student Research Programs <div>The Assistant Director for the Office of Undergraduate Research (OUR) at UNC Charlotte supports efforts and programming aimed at supporting faculty, staff, and students' participation in undergraduate research at UNC Charlotte. The Assistant Director will serve as the program manager for part of the university’s portfolio of undergraduate research programs, liaison for on-campus partners and events, and support student engagement in professional development opportunities. This position is responsible for implementing and managing digital tools to collect student applications, facilitate student research presentations, and support professional development activities that build essential career competencies among students.<br><br>Beyond these initiatives, the position will dedicate a portion of their time to building program engagement by delivering presentations, facilitating small group discussions, hosting drop-in support services, and providing individual coaching as needed. The ideal candidate will have a strong background in experiential learning, student engagement, and career development, with the ability to effectively manage programs and interact with student populations.</div><div><br></div><ul><li>Serve as the Assistant Director for OUR’s portfolio of research experiences for undergraduate students. This involves participating in student recruitment for undergraduate students; matching students with mentors based on the students’ discipline and areas of research; and creating and facilitating professional development training for students.</li><li>Provide support, training, and consulting, in conjunction with the Director, to faculty mentors working with students. Develop and maintain best practices in supporting students in the research process, implement mentoring agreements, creating the necessary training and development structures for the onboarding of students and faculty.</li><li>Manage the scholars program and process that includes process collateral, processes/policies, recruitment of student researchers, and program assessment. Will work in collaboration with the OUR team to meet these objectives.</li><li>Collaborates with the entire team on operations, budget, strategic planning, and assessment of program impact.</li><li>Will be responsible for teaching research internship courses.</li><li>Attends weekly staff meetings, training and planning meetings, and event team meetings.</li><li>Collaborates with the OUR team in order to plan and execute on-site events, research conferences and symposia, other related events, as well as campus-wide programs, and assists with communication of these initiatives.</li><li>Serves on Division and University committees and participates in Division and University-wide programs and events as appropriate.</li><li>Serve as a liaison for campus partners that support OUR student programming and channels of opportunity and external entities that are interested in collaborating on initiatives and innovation that increase the awareness of research in students.</li><li>Supervise student workers on student-focused awareness, communication, and branding efforts for undergraduate research across campus.</li><li>Collaborate with the director in strategically connecting to Colleges/Schools at Charlotte, working towards expanding access to research for students and removing barriers while expanding the network of mentors.</li><li>The Assistant Director will also attend local, regional, and national conferences to promote the university’s initiative, as appropriate.</li><li>Engage with students through clubs and organizations interested in learning about research, activating opportunities for students to attend conferences and symposiums.</li><li>Other duties as assigned.</li></ul> University College https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69626 2026-07-27T12:37:21-04:00 2026-07-27T12:37:22-04:00 Executive Director of Budget and Personnel <div>The Executive Director of Budget and Personnel works closely with the Vice Chancellor and Associate Vice Chancellor to develop the Division or Research’s annual operating budget; leads the division annual budget call process; reviews and advises senior leadership on budget requests and presents to the Vice Chancellor and Associate Vice Chancellor; works with senior leadership within decentralized business office operation to meet division and university strategic goals; monitors budget spending, communicates to Vice Chancellor and Associate Vice Chancellor on departmental and divisional budget; serves as DR’s internal controller and approver for budget management and business processes; ensures effective controls are in place and followed; develops and analyzes budget reports as required; manages and oversees the funding of internal grant programs; manages a team of direct report staff while accomplishing these duties; develops procedures that meet the business need of the units within the Division of Research; manages and oversees the Millennial Campus Business Office operation.</div><div><br>Executive Director serves as the Human Resources Manager and Liaison for the Division of Research staff (~92); HR liaison and support for DR staff; business continuity planning; initiates, reviews, and approves all HR processes for DR; manages, oversees, and troubleshoots as possible, in coordination with the campus HR Office, the annual performance evaluation system process for DR departments; maintains and manages HR files as a decentralized business office; works with HR Office as needed for SHRA and EHRA positions, including reviewing JCAT data, etc.; serves as the department timekeeper in KRONOS system, delegated authority for Personnel actions from Vice Chancellor; manages a team of direct report staff managing HR initiatives; conducts and oversees Division annual performance evaluation process to meet campus HR deadlines. Oversees campus-wide Postdoctoral Fellows Program hiring processes.</div><div><br>The Executive Director leads the Millennial Campus Business Office to manage and oversee the processing of all University Business Partner (UBP) legal agreements, invoicing and collection of financials; reporting of financials; manages a team of staff to provide customer service support for UBP’s; responsible for accounting/business standards and practices, policy interpretation and implementation, organization of work and verification of all.</div><div><br>The Executive Director serves as a member of the Senior Leadership team; supervises Budget and Personnel Team of six (6) staff; serves as a liaison for Division of Research to numerous external and internal partners as needed; provides and/or initiates written and verbal communications to implement and/or explain policies and procedures; attends campus research activities, business partner events, and miscellaneous events for DR; communicates decisions as they impact internal and external customers; manages the licensing rental agreements and invoicing process for Co-Lab entrepreneurial facility in uptown for external partners; and manages special projects as assigned by the Vice Chancellor and/or Associate Vice Chancellor in any area for the Division of Research.</div> Div of Research VC Office https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66430 2026-02-12T11:30:43-05:00 2026-02-12T11:30:44-05:00 Adjunct/Part-time Faculty; Accounting <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Turner School of Accountancy https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70147 2026-08-24T16:21:21-04:00 2026-08-24T16:21:22-04:00 FMT Building Trades Technician 1 <div>Duties include but are not limited to:</div><ul><li>Provide career tracks in trades such as caulking, coating, masonry, painting, plastering, and roofing, which encompass tasks ranging from routine and unskilled to highly skilled.</li><li>Apply methods and general procedures of relevant trades and maintenance techniques, while developing proficiency in procedures, techniques, tools, materials, and equipment appropriate to the trade.</li><li>Perform tasks including maintenance, repair, replacement, fabrication, installation, alteration, construction, and design of facility components. These tasks require skills in planning, problem-solving, attention to detail, teamwork, communication, and adherence to safety policies and regulations.</li><li>Serve as a helper to advanced and journey-level technicians, assisting them in daily job duties and gaining hands-on experience.</li><li>Work outdoors, often in elevated conditions. Applicants must be comfortable with heights.</li><li>Assist advanced and journey-level technicians with tasks performed from lifts, while learning to operate the lifts as part of the job responsibilities.</li><li>Ability to obtain a drone license as part of the position requirements to assist advanced and journey-level technicians with building and roof inspections</li></ul> FM General Trades Shop https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/67949 2026-05-07T12:50:03-04:00 2026-05-07T12:50:05-04:00 Salesforce Senior Developer <div>Salesforce Development &amp; Engineering</div><ul><li>Designs, develops, tests, and deploys complex Salesforce solutions using Apex, Lightning Web Components (LWC), Aura, SOQL/SOSL, and other platform technologies.</li><li>Builds scalable, reusable components and applies best practices for performance, security, and maintainability.</li><li>Serves as a technical expert for Salesforce development, setting direction for complex solution design, and resolving complex system issues that scale with the overall Salesforce architecture.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>Solution Design &amp; Technical Leadership</div><ul><li>Leads development efforts for complex Salesforce initiatives that span multiple clouds and integrated systems.</li><li>Translates business requirements into technical designs and implements solutions leveraging platform capabilities such as Flow, Platform Events, and asynchronous processing.</li><li>Ensures solutions align with enterprise architecture and data management practices.</li></ul><div><br>Integration &amp; Data Architecture</div><ul><li>Develops and maintains integrations between Salesforce and external systems using APIs and middleware platforms such as MuleSoft.</li><li>Ensures secure and reliable data exchange across systems and supports enterprise data architecture, including Data Cloud, where applicable.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>Advanced Capabilities (AI, Automation, Analytics)</div><ul><li>Designs and implements advanced Salesforce capabilities, including AI-driven features, automation, and analytics, to support enterprise use cases across the student and institutional lifecycle.</li></ul><div><br>Collaboration, Mentorship &amp; Architecture Alignment</div><ul><li>Collaborates with administrators, analysts, architects, and business stakeholders to deliver solutions aligned with University priorities.</li><li>Provides mentorship and technical guidance to other developers and contributes to knowledge sharing across the team.</li><li>Contributes to and influences enterprise architecture discussions and decisions, ensuring Salesforce solutions align with the broader University technology strategy.</li></ul> OneIT - Enterprise Applications https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66558 2026-02-23T00:00:55-05:00 2026-02-23T00:00:55-05:00 Post-Doctoral Fellow-Experimental Heat Transfer & Multiphase Flows <div>The candidate will work in a convergent team environment to conduct two-phase heat transfer and interfacial flow experiments, collaborate with modeling experts, and mentor graduate students. Prior experience in advanced imaging, sensing techniques, and scaling analysis is highly desired. The initial appointment is for one year with the potential to be extended to the second year based on mutual agreement.<br><br></div> Mechanical Engineering and Engineering Science https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70286 2026-09-01T07:52:48-04:00 2026-09-01T07:52:49-04:00 Academic Advisor I <div>The position is responsible for providing accurate academic advising to individuals and groups of current and potential undergraduate students in the College. Advisors proactively communicate with students who have poor mid-term grade performance or who have other risk factors for retention and/or success. Advisors assist in the management and operation of the CHHS Advising Center as determined by or in consultation with the Director and assist in preparing reports related to student enrollment and progression. The position contributes to the professional development of fellow college and unit-level advisors. On occasion, academic advisors are involved in creating and implementing student programs that focus on improving student success, retention, and graduation (i.e., College’s Learning Community, 49er Rebound, University Convocation, Transfer Student Program, Prospect for Success Program, etc.).</div><ul><li>Principles and Best Practices. Understand and effectively implement developmental advising principles and best practices that emphasize the advisor’s role in guiding students’ development of environmental and interpersonal awareness, problem-solving and decision-making skills, in addition to capacity for self-evaluation and behavioral adaptation. Defined as such, advising is essentially a teaching function and is an integral part of the University’s educational mission.</li><li>Curriculum Knowledge. Learning and remaining current on all relevant curriculum for the CHHS undergraduate educational programs, and for curricular areas more broadly beneficial to the students’ degree program.</li><li>Student Development. Assist students in developing the knowledge and skills needed to effectively explore, establish, and integrate their academic, career, and life goals. Assist students in developing plans for making progress towards these goals while attending UNC Charlotte and evaluating and charting one’s own progress toward those goals.</li><li>Understand and effectively develop students’ knowledge of university academic policies, regulations, guidelines, and deadlines and the curriculum requirements for the specific program(s) of study of the students being advised.</li><li>Have, and develop in students, awareness of curriculum requirements for other programs that may be relevant for the students being advised.</li><li>Remain current on all policy and curriculum changes.</li><li>Evaluate whether it is necessary to refer a student to another office depending on the nature of the student’s question/needs and make such referrals using appropriate protocols to ensure students’ needs are being met.</li><li>Curricular and Co-curricular Services and Engagement. Understand and effectively develop both students’ knowledge of and their motivations to pursue the full range of curricular and co-curricular support services and engagement activities available at the University.</li><li>Technology as Tools. Understand and exploit the capabilities of the suite of academic advising technology-based systems used at the University. Effectively use these advising systems to proactively engage students to ensure degree program persistence and timely graduation. Develop students’ abilities to use these systems and other relevant information to regularly evaluate one’s own progress toward academic goals and make appropriate behavioral and academic changes.</li><li>Maintain advising calendar, enter and update all notes on advising interactions, track and evaluate alert flags and kudos to engage with students following general and unit protocols.</li><li>Use Degree Works to evaluate students’ progress to degree.</li><li>Use of Banner Student Information System including selected INB screens.</li><li>Use the CONNECT platform to input and access information on students’ academic risk and track progress.</li><li>Orientation. Contribute to orientation programs for new students (transfer) that involve activities beyond individualized academic advising. May include presenting workshops and/or interacting with students individually or in groups to answer questions and/or facilitate discussion aimed at building student knowledge, awareness, and skills.</li><li>Outreach. Participate in recruitment events, providing information to and interacting with students and parents to answer questions and facilitate discussions aimed to build student knowledge, awareness, and skills.</li><li>Student Success Services. Receive and make referrals to other academic advising units and other campus offices associated with student success services.</li><li>Assist college recruitment efforts by facilitating the declaration process once a student has expressed intent and met qualifications.</li></ul> College of Health & Human Svc https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69676 2026-07-29T12:33:23-04:00 2026-07-29T12:33:23-04:00 Adjunct/Part-Time Faculty; Languages, Cultures and Translation <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Languages, Cultures, and Translation https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70199 2026-08-26T10:42:49-04:00 2026-08-26T10:42:50-04:00 Temporary Research Support Pool-Salaried <div>* Responsibilities may include: conducting experiments, collecting and summarizing data, and providing proper laboratory/study subject care.<br><br>* Duties will vary by departmental needs.</div> All departments https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/68639 2026-06-11T16:20:35-04:00 2026-06-11T16:20:36-04:00 Merchant Systems Analyst <ul><li>Merchant System Solution Technical Consultant - Serve as the subject matter expert in University payment card acceptance solutions. Maintain knowledge in merchant systems, payment applications, related business processes, PCI (Payment Card Industry) standards, and other policies and requirements. Use expertise to analyze departmental operational needs to anticipate problems, recommend compliant solutions or system modifications to achieve strategic goals, and operate within the bounds of established policy and procedures. Continuously work to improve card processing operations and respond to changing needs and technologies. Use creative and critical thinking to evaluate requests, issues, and/or merchant system problems and recommend compliant solutions. Consult, advise, and direct University staff across all levels (e.g., cashiers to departmental directors) with merchant system solutions.</li><li>Merchant Systems and Technology Management - Manage all University payment card acceptance operations, including POS terminals, payment card applications (i.e., creating/editing online storefronts and implementing third party integrations. Oversee and troubleshoot issues that arise with user access management for merchant systems and Point of Sale(POS) terminals. Assist Cashiers with the user access audit for merchant systems that includes First Data, American Express, and TouchNet. Resolve issues that might arise in the implementation of processing systems, reporting systems, or internal systems related to the payment process or changes dictated by PCI, NC OSC, UNC GA, and/or University policy. Serve as the lead technical liaison between campus merchants, colleagues, external resources, and OneIT on issues that arise involving payment card processing systems. Review and analyze annual merchant PCI validation documentation based on system and payment card security knowledge; follow up as needed to ensure accuracy of payment card processing systems. Work in conjunction with OneIT to oversee payment card system security. Assess and resolve unprecedented problems that require research and review of policy and procedures for the program or assigned components. Monitor merchant system databases, schedules, and reports. Analyzes software applications for their functionality and system requirements, in relation to our state contracted merchant services provider and supported payment gateways.</li><li>Third Party System Integrations - Communicate technical policies and procedures and necessary system implementation guidance to all campus merchants clearly and effectively. Analyze software applications for their functionality and merchant system requirements, in relation to our state contracted merchant services provider and supported payment gateways. Define scope, goals, tasks, deliverables, timelines, tools, and resources necessary to implement merchant system solutions, and ensure accountability. Serve as the lead technical consultant for campus merchants, colleagues, external resources, and OneIT on project teams involving payment card processing systems. Coordinate with acquiring banks, payment gateways, third-party services providers, and campus merchants to initiate setup, lead testing efforts, and ensure PCI compliant payment integrations.</li><li>Merchant Services and Data Analysis - Maintain and enhance positive professional working relationships with all merchant stakeholders to increase credibility for building support and reaching consensus when explaining and interpreting program policies and procedures within assigned area(s). Develop, conduct, and facilitate training on payment card processing systems to enhance campus merchant knowledge and department operations. Provide guidance and expertise to campus merchants on Merchant Services initiatives and develop staff’s knowledge of payment card processing from online technologies and gateway reports to point of sale equipment and device troubleshooting. Assess the needs of campus merchants, determine gaps, and recommend changes in payment card processing systems, procedures, workflow and work assignments to improve efficiency and effectiveness. Assist the Merchant Services Manager with data analysis, as needed. Assist the Cashiers and campus merchant staff with merchant reports, as needed.</li></ul><div><strong>Other Job Duties</strong></div><ul><li>Contributes to University payment card acceptance policies and procedures.</li><li>Contributes to the PCI DSS annual validation process.</li><li>Maintains process documentation and compliance timelines.</li></ul><div><br></div> Bursar's Office https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66904 2026-03-10T14:49:07-04:00 2026-03-10T14:49:08-04:00 Adjunct/Part Time Faculty; Counseling <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Counseling https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70290 2026-09-01T08:22:25-04:00 2026-09-01T08:22:26-04:00 Transportation Services Supervisor <ul><li>Supervise and lead staff on your shift to help ensure University and departmental standards and expectations are met</li><li>Manage employees’ work time and time off requests in time-keeping programs: KRONOS, Web Time Entry, and Humanity</li><li>Ensure proper staff coverage is scheduled for regular work shifts, chartered and fixed-route services, special events, activities, and functions</li><li>Set a do-as-I-do by your willingness to perform any task that you expect of your staff</li><li>Create performance plans that enforce University and departmental goals, and review each employee’s performance with them at the end of the cycle</li><li>Recruit, train, orient, and mentor new employees to set service and program expectations, including specific program information related to Niner Paratransit, Niner Rides, and Niner Transit, customer service, policies and regulatory standards, and other department-set priorities</li><li>Conduct disciplinary actions for employee performance issues in accordance with policies and procedures</li><li>Retrain and coach existing employees during incidences of poor performance to help keep expectations in focus</li><li>Serve as a main point of contact for our customers, both internal and external, and provide exceptional levels of service in everything you do</li><li>Monitor and track customer feedback to help ensure proper levels of service are being provided</li><li>Serve as the primary point-of-contact for customer inquiries, feedback, and complaints, and work with staff and department leadership to modify employee performance to better meet the community’s needs</li><li>Attend University-led informational sessions like SOAR, Admitted Student Day, and other related department interest sessions to provide program and service information to campus community members and their families</li><li>Explain policy, procedures, and regulatory-governance information to customers to better set expectations related to the transportation services our unit supports</li><li>Identify service issues, safety-related concerns, and areas of improvement, and make recommendations on how to better support our customers</li><li>Inspect all Transportation equipment and resources on campus to ensure proper levels of service are available to campus community members.</li><li>Advocate and promote transportation programs through your professional relationships and dealings with campus partners</li><li>Supervise all day-to-day operations, programs, and services supported by the Transportation unit, including Niner Transit, Niner Paratransit, e-scooters and pedal-assist bicycles, bicycle lockers, chartered and special event transportation requests, bus advertising, and all other transportation-related services.</li><li>Coordinate with the operator of Niner Transit regarding operational changes that may affect the campus community. Provide direction to vendor leadership to help ensure service levels remain adequate for campus needs.</li><li>Assist the Transportation Manager with monitoring the department’s preventive maintenance program. Transfer vehicles to and from maintenance facilities and other locations as needed.</li><li>Review and address all submitted DVIRs (Driver Vehicle Inspection Reports) and Vehicle Usage Log sheets to help manage departmental vehicle needs and report vehicle defects to the Transportation.</li><li>Conduct routine vehicle inspections to audit your employees’ inspections and to help ensure vehicle defects and issues are being properly reported</li><li>Find, collect, review, measure, transfer, and furnish data associated with Transportation services and report information to PaTS leadership to help guide program changes that may better serve the campus community</li><li>Create and maintain staff employee files, both personnel and disqualification (DQ) for CDL holders.</li><li>Create and maintain employee personnel files. Maintain employee DQ files that comply with federal regulations for DOT commercial drivers</li><li>Communicate with employees, peers, supervisors, and customers regarding program activities and updates</li><li>Plan, coordinate, and participate in quarterly safety meetings for employees, and share relevant information with teammates</li><li>Review and respond to emails and phone calls from coworkers and customers in a timely manner</li><li>Document customer eligibility information and ensure dates of eligibility correlate with member access to services and programs</li><li>Make recommendations on policies and procedures that would benefit the employees and customers of Parking and Transportation Services</li><li>Utilize computer programs to log passenger transfer requests and dispatch service vehicles according to schedule needs</li><li>Monitor chartered transportation services; serve as a replacement vehicle operator when schedule vacancies exist.</li><li>Monitor inventory of employees’ equipment, supplies, and uniforms to help ensure they are properly equipped to support the department’s programs and services</li><li>Monitor signage and advertisement materials promoting Transportation programs and services to ensure proper messaging is being communicated to the community. Report instances where materials should be updated or replaced</li></ul> Parking https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69710 2026-08-03T07:36:20-04:00 2026-08-03T07:36:20-04:00 Part-Time Faculty; Chemistry <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Chemistry https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/64776 2025-10-24T17:01:46-04:00 2025-10-24T17:01:47-04:00 Postdoctoral Fellow - ECE Y. Zhang <div>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs' aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees.<br><br>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions:<br><br>* the appointee was recently (within the last five years) awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.);<br>* the appointment is temporary;<br>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;<br>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;<br>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and<br>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.<br><br>As an EOE/AA employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained, the University of North Carolina at Charlotte encourages applications from all underrepresented groups. Applicants subject to criminal background check.</div> Electrical & Computer Engineering https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70202 2026-08-26T12:38:45-04:00 2026-08-26T12:38:46-04:00 Business Services Coordinator <div>The Business Services Coordinator is part of the Belk College of Business Finance &amp; Personnel Team and provides direct support to the Assistant Director and Assistant Dean of Finance and Personnel. This position, along with 4 other team members reporting to the Assistant Dean, is responsible for processing all financial transactions and personnel actions originating in the Belk College of Business, an organization with six academic departments, the Center for Real Estate, and more than 150 permanent faculty and staff positions.</div> College of Business https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/68671 2026-06-15T00:03:02-04:00 2026-06-15T00:03:02-04:00 Postdoctoral Fellow, Biological Sciences_Ren Lab <div>· To develop further the research skills required for their independent work, in preparation for an academic or research career</div><div>· Lead projects on epigenetic mechanisms and genome organization</div><div>· Publish their work in peer-reviewed journals</div><div>· Present their work in national and international conferences</div><div>· Write mentored and independent grants</div><div>· Mentor junior trainees in the lab.<br><br></div><div>* The appointee was recently awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.);</div><div>* The appointment is temporary;</div><div>* The appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;</div><div>* The appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;</div><div>* The appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and</div><div>* The appointee has freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.</div> Biological Sciences https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66911 2026-03-10T15:40:41-04:00 2026-03-10T15:40:42-04:00 Adjunct/Part-time Faculty; Reading and Elementary Education <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Reading & Elementary ED https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70306 2026-09-01T16:12:57-04:00 2026-09-01T16:12:58-04:00 Assistant Athletic Director of Compliance <div>This position is responsible for interpreting NCAA rules and regulations and ensuring that UNC Charlotte remains in compliance with those rules.</div><ul><li>Serve as the primary Athletics Compliance liaison to the institutional Scholarship/Financial Aid Office and athletics CFO.</li><li>Oversee administration, monitoring, and reconciliation of student-athlete financial aid, including awards, adjustments, scholarship equivalencies, cost of attendance, and outstanding balances.</li><li>Coordinate financial aid audits and ensure compliance with NCAA, conference, and institutional requirements.</li><li>Serve as the primary compliance administrator for the College Athlete Payment System (CAPS), overseeing the accuracy, reconciliation, monitoring, and timely reporting of required data.</li><li>Coordinate with the Athletics Business Office and other campus partners regarding revenue-sharing agreements, payments, roster information, and required CAPS certifications/attestations.</li><li>Monitor roster and revenue-sharing requirements and maintain appropriate internal controls and documentation.</li><li>Coordinate NCAA initial and continuing eligibility certification in partnership with the Athletics Academic Center, Registrar, and other campus offices.</li><li>Guide transfer eligibility and prepare NCAA waivers as assigned.</li><li>Coordinate collection and submission of NCAA APR, GSR, and other required NCAA, conference, and institutional reports.</li><li>Serve as the primary compliance contact for assigned sport programs and provide rules interpretations and education to coaches, staff, and student-athletes.</li><li>Investigate potential NCAA violations, research applicable precedent and penalties, and prepare required NCAA/conference reporting.</li><li>Monitor and review compliance documentation, including CARA logs, recruiting activities, participation records, and other required forms and reports to ensure adherence to NCAA, conference, and institutional requirements</li><li>Assist with implementation of NCAA legislation, College Sports Commission requirements, and other regulatory changes; perform additional duties as assigned by the Senior Associate Athletic Director for Governance and Regulatory Affairs.</li></ul> Compliance https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69823 2026-08-11T00:00:47-04:00 2026-08-11T00:00:49-04:00 Postdoctoral Fellow-Instrumented airfield pavements-Durable/sustainable concrete materials <div>The successful candidate will work on several ongoing and upcoming research projects supporting an improved understanding of concrete materials, mixtures, and testing technologies that advance the durability, sustainability, and constructability of pavement and structures. A key focus of the work will be supporting the installation, deployment, and development of instrumentation at the Charlotte Douglas International Airport (CLT) as part of a project funded by the Federal Aviation Administration’s (FAA) Airport Concrete Pavement Technology Program (ACPTP). This project will facilitate the development of a digital twin and provide extensive, long-term data to enhance the understanding of pavement performance and inform maintenance decisions. Information will be used to inform and enhance FAA’s existing pavement design tool FAARFIELD and other software programs, design and construction advisory circulars (ACs) such as FAA’s AC 150/5320-6G Airport Pavement Design and Evaluation and AC 150 5320-10H, and airport and stakeholder partner practices in design, construction, operation, and maintenance of airfield pavements.</div><div>Work outside the ACPTP project will include field and laboratory activities to support use of novel materials, tests, and analysis approaches for highway and aviation concrete materials and mixtures. Ongoing and upcoming research projects that the successful candidate will support are funded by the North Carolina Department of Transportation, the Federal Aviation Administration, the Federal Highway Administration, and private entities. Opportunities to engage with and support the growth of the Charlotte Aviation Innovation and Research (AIR) Institute will also be provided as part of the position’s duties.</div><div>The successful candidate will work with Dr. Tara Cavalline and several doctoral, masters, and undergraduate students in a collaborative, supportive team environment. The candidate will engage with other researchers at UNC Charlotte and other universities as part of their project-related activities, and will assist in mentoring, training, and guiding graduate students executing and publishing research. UNC Charlotte and the AIM Lab team are committed to fostering an environment of support and inclusivity for all individuals.<br><br></div><div>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs' aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees.<br><br>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions:<br><br>* the appointee was recently (within the last eight years) awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.);<br>* the appointment is temporary;<br>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;<br>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;<br>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and<br>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.</div> Albert School of Construction https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69058 2026-06-30T11:40:34-04:00 2026-06-30T11:40:35-04:00 Adjunct Faculty/Part-Time Lecturer; Electrical & Computer Engineering <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br><em>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, disability, gender identity or sexual orientation.&nbsp;</em></div> Electrical & Computer Engineering https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/64969 2025-11-05T15:32:35-05:00 2025-11-05T15:32:35-05:00 Postdoctoral Fellow, Biological Sciences <div>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship<strong> </strong>designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. The primary duty and responsibility of the postdoc will be to lead projects and mentor junior trainees in the lab.</div><div>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions:</div><div>* the appointee was recently (within the last four years) awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent</div><div>doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.);</div><div>* the appointment is temporary;</div><div>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;</div><div>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;</div><div>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and</div><div>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.<br><br></div><div>As an EOE/AA employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained, the University of North Carolina at Charlotte encourages applications from all underrepresented groups. Applicants subject to criminal background check.</div> Biological Sciences https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70230 2026-08-28T12:02:24-04:00 2026-08-28T12:02:25-04:00 Building Operations Assistant <div>Assist with the library daily operations. Duties include, recycling, painting, changing lights, replacing ceiling tiles, mail room coverage, moving furniture, book donations pickups and deliveries on and off campus, cleaning furniture, repairing furniture, responding to emergency leaks, carpet spot cleaning and replacement, window cleaning, elevator track cleaning, office cleaning, special event setup, computer and printer setup, maintain safe and well organized storage areas, relocate personnel within the building and assist with fixed assets. Clean and maintains all equipment used to upkeep the Library. Other duties as assigned.</div> Library https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66915 2026-03-10T15:46:45-04:00 2026-03-10T15:46:46-04:00 Adjunct/Part-time Faculty; Office of School & Community Partnerships <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> School & Community Partnership https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70325 2026-09-02T15:08:40-04:00 2026-09-02T15:08:41-04:00 Assistant Director for Education Abroad <div>The Assistant Director for Education Abroad assists the Director and Associate Director in the oversight of daily operations in the Office of Education Abroad, exchange and affiliate program student advising, new exchange, affiliate, and hybrid program development and implementation, updating and implementing pre-departure and re-entry orientations for students, contributing to the unit’s risk management, expanding the number and variety of students participating in study abroad opportunities, campus outreach and training.</div><ul><li>Serve as the advising manager for all education abroad programs and research, and remain well informed about the many program opportunities available to our students, including study abroad, internship, work, and volunteer options; work closely with and maintain appropriate relationships with faculty and academic advisers; and collaborate with the Director of the office in developing and implementing new programs.</li><li>Meet with academic departments and advising units to conduct annual program review and evaluation.</li><li>Oversee planning and implementation of pre-departure orientation, re-entry and scholarship programs. Organize the bi-annual Education Abroad Fairs, the annual Photo Contest, and other promotional and informational events.</li><li>Administer the application and registration procedures for designated exchange programs and non-UNC Charlotte programs.</li><li>Maintain and develop web-based information and materials associated with study abroad programs such as the office website and social media platforms, database systems maintenance, and creating recruitment and advising literature for designated programs.</li><li>Supervise the Education Abroad Advisor, Study Abroad Ambassadors, and office interns.</li><li>Advise students on exchange, affiliate, hybrid, and direct enrollment program opportunities.</li><li>Process grades and coursework records for non-faculty-led programs.</li></ul> Charlotte Global https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70039 2026-08-18T13:43:35-04:00 2026-08-18T13:43:36-04:00 Marketing Technology Manager <div>The Marketing Technology Manager is part IT specialist and part integrated marketing specialist who serves on the Marketing and Communications (MarComm) team of UNC Charlotte's Digital and Professional Learning (DPL). The professional must understand marketing tactics such as search engine optimization, search engine marketing, content marketing, social media marketing, and email marketing, as well as the technology we use to implement them.</div><div><br></div><div>The position will support the development and maintenance of the unit's web presence, including the creation of new web pages and landing sites on the WordPress platform and lead governance and upkeep on existing and legacy DPL websites and pages. The position will assist with the migration of websites and pages currently on the Drupal CMS platform to WordPress.</div><div><br></div><div>Additionally, the position will deliver marketing automation as the administrator of our marketing automation tool (Salesforce Marketing Cloud), including one-off email campaigns and orchestrating automated communication journeys.</div><div><br></div><div>Finally, the position will help to measure the effectiveness and results of executed campaigns through analytical tools such as Google Analytics (GA4) and Salesforce. The position will be responsible for integrations and maintenance of products that the marketing team uses for campaign research and execution.</div> DPL-Prg Mgmt & Learner Engagement https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69080 2026-07-01T00:01:22-04:00 2026-09-04T13:05:42-04:00 Temporary Clerical Pool-Hourly <div>* Responsibilities may include: general office reception, mail distribution, records processing, data entry, filing, correspondence, appointment scheduling, general accounting, etc.<br><br>* Duties will vary by departmental needs.</div> All departments https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/65117 2025-11-14T12:01:44-05:00 2025-11-14T12:01:44-05:00 Postdoctoral Fellow <div>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs' aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees.<br><br>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions:<br><br>* the appointee was recently (within the last eight years) awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.);<br>* the appointment is temporary;<br>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;<br>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;<br>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and<br>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.<br><br>The employee will carry out research in optical materials especially for applications in high power lasers. Specifically, they will work on creating micro or nano structures on surfaces optical substrates and optical fibers using micro-optics facilities at UNCC. The work may be carried out in association with the Office of Naval Research, Naval Research Lab, or other partnering institutions.<br><br>The post-doc will be engaged in developing micro-fabrication processes on glass and fibers for anti-reflectivity with applications in high laser power transmission, and photonic crystal fiber couplers. They will also work in the lab to develop processes to create microstructures in varieties of materials for the purpose of anti-reflection. They will characterize the samples, analyze the data, and interpret the results. They will also study the relevant literature and develop theoretical explanations or models for the results. They may also work on writing proposals for external funding. They will also prepare reports for briefing funding agents.<br><br>The post-doc will be allowed to act independently, but will receive close guidance and direction from Prof. Aggarwal and Prof. Hutchens so as to maintain a focused research objective.<br><br>As an EOE/AA employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained, the University of North Carolina at Charlotte encourages applications from all underrepresented groups. Applicants subject to criminal background check.</div> Physics and Optical Science https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70247 2026-08-31T10:29:09-04:00 2026-08-31T10:29:09-04:00 Police Officer <div>This position performs law enforcement work at a University campus or other State facility. With full powers of arrest, positions enforce the laws of the State and the United States Government. Positions have law enforcement authority ranging from traffic control to criminal investigations within a limited geographic jurisdiction. Work involves patrolling an assigned area for response to complaints or calls and for detection of violations in the enforcement of State laws and applicable regulations. Duties include apprehending observed violators committing offenses, investigating violations or complaints in order to assess and handle cases, collection and preservation of evidence and the chain of custody, testifying in court proceedings, and performing related work.</div><ul><li>Protects life and property through the enforcement of laws and regulations.</li><li>Conducts preliminary and follow-up criminal investigations.</li><li>Conducts detentions, interviews, and arrests in accordance with the law and departmental directives.</li><li>Communicates with others by vocal and written means. Relays clear, intelligible messages through proper words and body language.</li><li>Complies with all departmental directives and university policies. Completes all assigned tasks and duties.</li></ul> Police and Public Safety https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70058 2026-08-19T14:34:50-04:00 2026-08-19T14:34:51-04:00 Associate Director for HRL Content Operations <div><strong>Essential Job Duties:<br></strong><br></div><ul><li>Lead and oversee the development and execution of Housing and Residence Life (HRL) content strategy aligned with departmental, divisional, and university priorities</li><li>Plan, coordinate, and implement marketing campaigns across print, digital, web, social media, and video platforms</li><li>Serve as the primary lead for HRL communications, including crisis and emergency messaging</li><li>Develop, write, and edit clear, concise, and engaging content for internal and external audiences in alignment with AP style and university branding standards</li><li>Manage multiple projects simultaneously, including establishing timelines, tracking deliverables, and ensuring deadlines are met</li><li>Create and manage content calendars and workflows for marketing and communications initiatives</li><li>Coordinate design and production of promotional materials and collaborate with vendors for printing and branded merchandise</li><li>Maintain and update web content, including pages, images, and navigation</li><li>Lead social media strategy and execution across platforms such as Instagram, Facebook, YouTube, and emerging channels</li><li>Coordinate photography and videography efforts, including scripting and storyboard development for video content</li><li>Build and maintain collaborative relationships with University Communications, Student Affairs Marketing, and other campus partners</li><li>Consult and advise departmental stakeholders on marketing strategies and communication approaches</li><li>Assess effectiveness of content strategies using metrics, feedback, and best practices, and implement continuous improvements</li><li>Train and guide staff on communication tools, marketing technologies, and best practices</li></ul> Housing and Residence Life https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/67133 2026-03-23T12:24:36-04:00 2026-03-23T12:24:36-04:00 Postdoctoral Fellow, Biological Sciences_Song <div>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs' aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees.<br><br>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions:<br><br>* the appointee was recently (within the last eight years) awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.);<br>* the appointment is temporary;<br>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;<br>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;<br>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and<br>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.<br><br><br>As an EOE/AA employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained, the University of North Carolina at Charlotte encourages applications from all underrepresented groups. Applicants subject to criminal background check.</div> Biological Sciences https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70329 2026-09-02T16:24:30-04:00 2026-09-02T16:24:31-04:00 Sports Performance Coach for Baseball and Softball <div>Provide an environment where student athletes can succeed both academically and athletically in softball and baseball. Coach student-athletes in strength and conditioning for softball and baseball. Assist with game-day duties, camps, and recruiting for both sports.</div> Athletics Admin/Support https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69152 2026-07-02T15:56:38-04:00 2026-07-02T15:56:39-04:00 Director of Marketing & Revenue Generation <ul><li>Develop and execute a comprehensive integrated marketing plan for Charlotte Football with an emphasis on maximizing revenue, increasing attendance, and creating a captivating game day experience.</li><li>Collaborate with Creative, Ticket Operations, Ticket Sales, Fundraising, Strategic Communications, Sponsorship, and other campus and auxiliary partnerships to create integrated marketing campaigns with a focus on maximizing engagement, sales and revenue generation.</li><li>Support the department’s email marketing and automation strategy</li><li>Support fundraising communication efforts to help increase membership and fundraising revenue.</li><li>Maintain a good understanding of marketing and game presentation best practices and new technologies.</li></ul> Athletics Admin/Support https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66424 2026-02-12T11:06:47-05:00 2026-02-12T11:06:47-05:00 Adjunct/Part-Time Faculty; Finance <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Finance https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70259 2026-08-31T14:19:03-04:00 2026-08-31T14:19:04-04:00 Assistant Director of Transfer and Adult Student Advising <div>The Transfer and Adult Student Advisor supports prospective and enrolled students with prior college credit, including transfer and adult learners, by providing comprehensive academic advising and transition support. This position helps students understand how their credits apply, explore academic options, and identify coursework that supports a seamless transition into their intended major and timely progression to graduation. The advisor also plays a key role in coordinating initiatives that strengthen transfer and adult student pathways across campus and with external partners.</div><div><br></div><ul><li>Provide academic advising to prospective and enrolled transfer and adult students through in-person, virtual, and off-site modalities.</li><li>Assist students in understanding how prior credit applies to degree requirements, including general education, major prerequisites, and course sequencing.</li><li>Guide students in exploring academic options and selecting courses prior to enrollment to support a seamless transition into upper-division coursework within their intended major.</li><li>Support students in navigating academic policies and processes, including change of major requirements and institutional expectations after transfer.</li><li>Provide supplemental advising support to enrolled students with transfer credit, including guidance on the academic petition process for credit reevaluation.</li><li>Support transition for newly enrolled students by connecting them to appropriate campus resources and services.</li><li>Provide advising that is responsive to the needs of adult learners, including students balancing work, family responsibilities, and returning to college after time away.</li><li>Maintain accurate and timely documentation of advising interactions in accordance with university policies and best practices.</li><li>Coordinate and deliver programming, workshops, and presentations that support transfer and adult student transition and success.</li><li>Participate in outreach efforts, including community college visits, campus events, and virtual engagement opportunities.</li><li>Assist in the development of materials and resources that support student understanding of transfer pathways and academic planning.</li><li>Contribute to initiatives that support community building and persistence among transfer and adult students.</li><li>Lead or co-lead departmental and university initiatives related to transfer, adult learner success, and pathway development.</li><li>Serve as a liaison to designated campus partners and/or external institutions (e.g., community colleges) to support transfer pathways and reduce credit loss.</li><li>Coordinate components of transfer and/or adult student initiatives that require collaboration across units, including pathway development, transition programming, and joint outreach efforts.</li><li>Serve on committees or working groups and contribute to the development and implementation of strategies that support student success.</li><li>Engage in ongoing professional development and contribute to the professional learning of the advising team through sharing of best practices, resources, or training.</li></ul> Univ Transfer/Adult Stu Ctr https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70125 2026-08-21T15:53:58-04:00 2026-08-24T13:12:55-04:00 Library Business Officer <div>The Library Business Officer (LBO) plays a pivotal role in the financial, personnel, and operational leadership of the library. As a key member of the Dean’s leadership team, the Library Business Officer provides strategic oversight, guidance, communication, operational strategy, and decision-making in the areas of financial management and human resources. This position ensures that all business activities align with university policies and strategic goals while fostering collaboration across departments and with stakeholders from divisions across the university. The LBO is expected to have substantive knowledge of the goals of the library and university and an awareness of broader higher education trends to ensure strategic alignment.<br><br></div><div><strong>Financial Management and Business Operations</strong><br><br></div><div>The LBO manages comprehensive financial operations and business administration, ensuring efficient and compliant resource management. This includes budgetary planning, forecasting, and supervising business staff. The LBO collaborates with university offices to optimize financial performance, implement innovative processes, and oversee external grants and contracts.<br><br></div><ul><li><strong>Budget Oversight and Planning:</strong> Lead strategic financial planning and forecasting for the library’s $15M budget, developing long-range objectives aligned with institutional goals.</li><li>F<strong>inancial Reporting and Analysis: </strong>Prepare and reconcile records for multiple funding sources, providing insights on trends and status to support sound decision-making.</li><li><strong>Fund Maintenance and Transactions:</strong> Oversee account reconciliation, budget projections, revisions, and purchasing approvals.</li><li><strong>Procurement and Operations: </strong>Manage fiscal functions including accounting, travel, expense reimbursement, contract review, and policy enforcement.</li><li><strong>Grants and Contracts Management: </strong>Direct post-award management for external grants and research funding in coordination with principal investigators.</li><li><strong>Personnel and Payroll Management: </strong>Monitor salary allocations and vacant positions while ensuring accurate payroll transactions for EHRA and SHRA roles.</li><li><strong>Compliance and Process Improvement:</strong> Interpret state and university policies, design efficient internal workflows, and train staff on financial systems and tools.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Human Resources Management<br></strong><br></div><div>The LBO oversees human resources functions for approximately 110 FTE, managing the full employee lifecycle and ensuring policy compliance. This includes supervising business staff and handling personnel issues for faculty, staff, and student employees.</div><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>HR Leadership:</strong> Oversee recruitment, hiring, onboarding, promotion, and retention for faculty, staff, student, and temporary employee classifications, ensuring procedural accuracy and equity.</li><li><strong>Compensation and Benefits: </strong>Administer salary, benefits, leave, and workplace accommodations in compliance with federal, state, and university HR policies.</li><li><strong>Supervision and Workforce Planning: </strong>Lead the library business team, with responsibilities related to budget and human resources management, ensuring effective talent management and compliance with university policies.</li><li><strong>Issue Resolution and Confidentiality:</strong> Collaborate with supervisors and university HR to mediate personnel disputes and disciplinary matters in a timely manner, while maintaining strict confidentiality and alignment with institutional guidelines.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Data Analysis and Strategic Decision-Making<br></strong><br></div><div>The LBO uses data analysis to inform strategic decisions, improve operational efficiency, and manage risk. This role involves auditing, policy research, and providing expert advice to leadership on resource allocation.<strong><br></strong><br></div><ul><li><strong>Data-Driven Recommendations:</strong> Analyze financial and personnel data to provide recommendations that support strategic objectives and resource allocation. Conduct research on university policies to resolve complex financial, operational, and human resources issues and develop improved financial processes.</li><li><strong>Audit and Risk Management: </strong>Oversee internal and external audits, address findings, and implement risk mitigation strategies and corrective actions.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><strong>Leadership and Stakeholder Engagement<br></strong><br></div><div>As a member of the Dean’s leadership team, the LBO influences long-term financial, personnel, and operational strategies. The LBO acts as a proxy for the Dean in approving key business decisions, allowing executive leadership to focus on broader institutional priorities. The LBO serves as the primary liaison between the library and campus partners (e.g., Academic Affairs, HR, Internal Audit, Research) to ensure operational alignment with university systems and procedures.</div><div><br></div><ul><li><strong>Strategic Advising and Policy Leadership: </strong>Provide input to the Dean on resource stewardship and lead the establishment or revision of internal operational policies. Prepare analysis and reports in response to complex inquiries.</li><li><strong>University Liaison:</strong> Facilitate communication with key university offices, representing the library in institutional discussions and advocating for resource needs.</li></ul> Library https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/67665 2026-04-20T08:05:55-04:00 2026-04-20T08:05:56-04:00 Adjunct/Part-Time Faculty; Global Studies <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Global Studies https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70348 2026-09-04T14:23:04-04:00 2026-09-04T14:23:05-04:00 Award Management Specialist <div><strong>Professional Knowledge, Investigator Support &amp; Financial Analysis</strong></div><ul><li>Provide comprehensive sponsored project oversight and serve as the primary point of contact for PIs seeking support for post-award management activities.</li><li>Perform financial analysis of award spending, calculate burn rates, and provide highly accurate spending projections to guide research planning.</li><li>Collect and analyze financial data in preparation for monthly spending reports to PIs, providing relevant expenditure projections and an ongoing review of post-award activity.</li><li>In collaboration with department staff, complete thorough monthly reconciliations on grant awards and review results directly with Principal Investigators (PIs).</li><li>Work with PIs and campus administrators to solve problems, answer questions, and give guidance on post-award activities and other grants management topics.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Project Administration &amp; Lifecycle Management</strong></div><ul><li>Assist and provide guidance to faculty and unit-level staff on the financial management and tracking of awards, including but not limited to purchasing, travel, hiring of project personnel, reporting, and sub-award interactions.</li><li>Plan and execute award kickoff meetings and quarterly award meetings with PIs and department staff to review project status and financial health.</li><li>Manage overall budgets and reporting timelines throughout the entire lifecycle of the award, ensuring no deadlines are missed.</li><li>Collaboratively design, draft, and develop formal budget revisions and re-budgeting scenarios for active awards, aligning revised spending plans with project milestones.</li><li>Provide active support and guidance to department staff on institutional procedures and systems required to process financial transactions on grant accounts successfully.</li><li>Review award transactions, ensuring all charges are allowable, allocable, and reasonable.</li></ul><div><br><strong>Compliance, Information Analysis &amp; Decision-Making</strong></div><ul><li>Provide financial oversight, financial and administrative compliance guidance on awards, and award management support regarding sponsor and institutional policies, procedures, and guidelines.</li><li>Track and monitor cost-share commitments to ensure the institution meets its documented obligations.</li><li>Review, coordinate, and submit sponsor prior approval requests, Assumptions of Risk, and other administrative amendments.</li><li>Work as the lead mediator when financial non-compliance is identified. Collaborate with department staff to investigate the root cause, and partner with the Research Accounting and Financial Compliance Team to implement corrective action plans.</li><li>Oversee monthly expense reporting, projections, and formal closeouts. Assist faculty and unit-level staff with timely award closeouts, work to clear remaining encumbrances, and coordinate with the Research Accounting and Financial Compliance Team to submit final financial reports (FFRs).</li></ul><div><br><strong>Compliance, Information Analysis &amp; Decision-Making</strong></div><ul><li>Maintain strong, positive relationships with faculty and staff, all units within the Division of Research, and sponsor agencies to facilitate the work of research administration at the University.</li><li>Promote and maintain close, effective working relationships between the teams within the Office of Sponsored Research Administration.</li><li>Work with colleagues and team members to jointly solve questions and challenges in their daily work, responding to inquiries in a timely, professional manner and providing consistently excellent support.</li><li>Monitor changes to sponsor policies and procedures and assist with communicating key changes to campus research administrators and investigators.</li></ul><div><br></div><div>Other duties as assigned.</div> Off Sponsored Research Admin (SRA) https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69287 2026-07-09T08:47:24-04:00 2026-07-09T08:47:25-04:00 Adjunct/Part-Time Faculty; Civil & Environmental Engineering <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Civil and Environmental Engr https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66426 2026-02-12T11:20:39-05:00 2026-02-12T11:20:39-05:00 Adjunct/Part-time Faculty; Marketing <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Marketing https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70266 2026-08-31T15:14:21-04:00 2026-08-31T15:14:21-04:00 Assistant Director of Business Honors Program <div>The Assistant Director of the Business Honors Program implements career and professional development programming and educational strategies to advance honors education in the Belk College of Business. The Business Honors Program is a special educational and professional development program supported by the Niblock Student Center in collaboration with the Director of the Business Honors Program, the Associate Director of Professional Development, and other Honors College faculty and staff. The Assistant Director of the Business Honors Program will coordinate program admissions efforts, create and implement student programming suitable for an \"honors experience,\" and instruct Honors courses in the Belk College of Business, including sections of BUSN 1101 and 1701.<br>The Assistant Director of the Business Honors Program manages admissions, participant records, communications, and selection processes while serving as a liaison to key campus partners. The role provides instruction for designated business honors course sections, advises student leaders, and coordinates program events and engagement opportunities. The Assistant Director also mentors students and cultivates connections that support their academic, professional, and personal development.</div> Undergraduate Programs https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70133 2026-08-24T08:33:53-04:00 2026-08-24T08:33:54-04:00 Support Services Associate <ul><li>Deliver vendor orders as applicable to campus departments</li><li>Pick up and deliver surplus property as required by campus end-using departments</li><li>Organize and stage surplus property in the warehouse to maintain a neat work environment</li><li>Update systems and maintain records as required and directed by management</li><li>Assist in the annual verification of fixed asset inventories completed by campus departments</li><li>Interact with vendors and customers as required to answer questions and provide a high level of customer service</li></ul> Warehouse Operations https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/67729 2026-04-23T13:36:48-04:00 2026-04-23T13:36:49-04:00 Postdoctoral Fellow in Industrial & Systems Engineering <div>The Postdoctoral Fellow will conduct advanced research in deep learning, with a focus on optimization design and/or the application of deep learning methods to complex real-world problems. The position aims to develop novel algorithms, improve model efficiency and performance, and translate theoretical advances into practical solutions across relevant domains. The Fellow will contribute to scholarly publications, and support ongoing research initiatives with the supervisor.<br><br>A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs' aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees.<br><br>Postdoc appointments are characterized by all the following conditions:<br><br>* the appointee was recently (within the last eight years) awarded a Ph.D.<br>* the appointment is temporary;<br>* the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship;<br>* the appointment is viewed as preparatory for a full-time academic and/or research career;<br>* the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and<br>* the appointee has the freedom and is expected to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment.<br><br><br>As an EOE/AA employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained, the University of North Carolina at Charlotte encourages applications from all underrepresented groups. Applicants subject to criminal background check.</div> Dept of Industrial & Systems Engin https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70354 2026-09-04T15:54:27-04:00 2026-09-04T15:54:28-04:00 Benefits Consultant <div>The Benefits Consultant Generalist provides adaptable, cross‑functional support across core benefits functions. This role delivers consultations and case management for leaves of absence, retirement processing, onboarding and offboarding activities, and related benefits administration. The consultant manages high‑volume inquiries, time‑sensitive casework, and ensures accurate application of federal, state, and university policies. By providing coverage during peak workloads, staff absences, and overlapping deadlines, the generalist helps maintain timely, compliant service and consistent support for employees throughout the employment lifecycle.<br><br><strong><br>Leave of Absence (LOA) – 30%</strong></div><ul><li>Guide LOA options, eligibility, benefits, and required processes.</li><li>Review applications and medical documentation, determine eligibility, and complete forms, notifications, and system entries.</li><li>Consult with employees on continuation, conversion/portability, waivers, and accelerated benefits.</li><li>Coordinate with Employee Relations when appropriate.</li><li>Monitor case data for accuracy, compliance, timekeeping, and billing; produce related reports.</li><li>Support additional LOA tasks as assigned.</li></ul><div><strong>Retirement – 30%</strong></div><ul><li>Consult employees on retirement options, eligibility, and benefit calculations for:<ul><li>Teachers’ and State Employees’ Retirement System (TSERS), including Law Enforcement Officer (LEO) benefits</li><li>Optional Retirement Plan (ORP)</li><li>Phased Retirement</li><li>Incentive programs such as the Faculty Incentive Retirement Program (FRIP)</li></ul></li><li>Guide related retirement benefits, including retiree health insurance, Medicare Parts A and B, NC Flex, and UNC System benefits.</li><li>Consult on supplemental retirement plans (UNC System 403(b), 457(b), NC 401(k)), including contribution limits, plan rules, and enrollment changes.</li><li>Process retirement forms, documentation, communications, and system entries; ensure accuracy and timely completion.</li><li>Support additional retirement tasks such as pension‑spiking reviews, special separation allowance calculations, and presentations.</li></ul><div><strong>Onboarding / Offboarding – 30%</strong></div><ul><li>Conduct orientations and benefits consultations for prospective, new, and transferring employees.</li><li>Assist with benefits enrollment, documentation, and platform tasks, including compliance with mandatory retirement plan elections.</li><li>Guide termination‑related benefits, including continuation/portability, COBRA, and vendor contacts.</li><li>Monitor separation reporting, end deductions, and process adjustments or exception requests as needed.</li><li>Support Reduction in Force (RIF) actions, including Discontinued Service Retirement (DSR) requests for eligible TSERS participants, employee consultations, and required payroll and platform updates.</li></ul><div><strong>Other Benefits Support – 10%</strong></div><ul><li>Provide general benefits support, including processing HR service cases through HR Connect.</li><li>Research and interpret benefits policies and collaborate with internal partners as needed.</li><li>Maintain process documentation and support process improvements.</li><li>Process qualifying life events and related system entries.</li><li>Assist with open enrollment, events, and other benefits duties as assigned.</li></ul> Benefits https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70268 2026-08-31T15:31:37-04:00 2026-08-31T15:31:38-04:00 Senior Contract Officer <ul><li>Independently review and negotiate terms and conditions of complex award documents and contractual agreements with various sponsors to ensure compliance with policies, federal and state laws, and sponsor guidelines.</li><li>Draft and negotiate agreements, including those for technical data and intellectual property rights, publication rights, payment terms, title to equipment, and travel regulations.</li><li>Write and review standard and complex agreements, subawards, and consulting agreements. Handle exceptions for Request for Proposal (RFP) documents.</li><li>Collaborate with Principal Investigator and department administrators to ensure timely and compliant processing of all agreement types.</li><li>Under minimal supervision, prepare correspondence and process requests to sponsors for modifications to existing agreements.</li><li>Ensure modifications are managed efficiently and in accordance with the sponsor guidelines.· Collaborate with the various offices in the Division of Research, Office of Legal Affairs, and other relevant offices to address compliance issues.</li><li>Work closely with senior negotiators and direct supervisors to ensure all agreements comply with relevant laws, policies, and procedures, including those of the UNC System and sponsor requirements.</li><li>Stay up to date with changing regulatory requirements and policies affecting sponsored research.</li><li>Maintain and manage all negotiation-related documentation in the award management database and negotiations tracking system, ensuring records are updated and accurately reflect the current status of each negotiation.</li><li>Ensure timely processing of all negotiations and keep all parties informed of the status.</li><li>Assist management with implementing performance metrics and improving negotiation tracking systems.</li><li>Provide expert advice on contract administration and negotiation best practices.</li><li>Assist supervisors in training and mentoring other colleagues.</li><li>Support less experienced staff by making recommendations on complex and non-routine contract issues.</li><li>Provide guidance and mentorship to less experienced contract negotiators on documentation management.</li><li>Develop and maintain strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including faculty, staff, and sponsors.</li></ul> Office of Research Services https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/69410 2026-07-14T16:05:28-04:00 2026-07-14T16:05:29-04:00 HRIS Business Systems Analyst <div>The primary purpose of this position is to provide production troubleshooting, operational support, and system subject-matter expertise for the functional users of Banner HR (HR/Payroll/Position Control modules) and other ancillary systems. This includes managing system integrations and 3rd-party vendor relationships, researching and resolving problems, and providing systems analysis and project management for implementations and process improvements.<br><br></div><ul><li>Provide production troubleshooting and operational support for all HR/Payroll systems and 3rd-party interfaces, including Banner HR, compensation, benefits, payroll, position control modules, PeopleAdmin, recruiting, position &amp; performance management, Law Logix, and HR/Payroll data feeds to other university or external systems.</li><li>Lead and/or contribute to HR/Payroll projects. This includes recommending solutions, gathering and documenting requirements for new solutions, testing solutions and system configuration changes, establishing requirements and data mapping for reports and interfaces, and data cleanup as needed.</li><li>Develop reports and fulfill data requests for HR/Payroll/Position Control data. Prioritize report requests and coordinate development &amp; testing with departmental reporting team members. Serve as the liaison between the report developer and campus partners.</li><li>Complete testing for Banner HR system upgrades, patches, or configuration changes.</li><li>Write and maintain system requirements, specifications, and process documentation.</li></ul> OneIT - Financial Systems Support https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/66429 2026-02-12T11:27:54-05:00 2026-02-12T11:27:55-05:00 Adjunct / Part-time Faculty; Economics <div>UNC Charlotte is accepting applications for adjunct or part-time faculty. These positions are temporary and usually involve teaching a particular course or courses for a particular semester. These positions are temporary and do not accrue benefits.<br><br>Responsibilities could include: Developing and/or updating course syllabus, preparation of lectures, projects, or other student assignments, meeting all designated class periods, maintaining regularly scheduled office hours, grading student assignments and exams in a timely manner, submitting completed mid-semester progress reports and final grades according to University regulations.<br><br>The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an Equal Opportunity Employer and an ADVANCE Institution that strives to create an academic climate in which the dignity of all individuals is respected and maintained. Women, minorities, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, protected veteran status, or disability. The chosen applicant is subject to a criminal background check.</div> Economics"
